[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229681 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a735c772-af97-5eea-b8e9-6a8dfb90fefa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v^{2} = \\frac{2.3 \\cdot 10^{18}}{r} + (1.6 \\cdot 10^{7})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228268} {:answer/id #uuid "ce5e64d1-8193-5c2a-aa97-28a6bbd12e4b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v^{2} = \\frac{2.3 \\cdot 10^{18}}{r^{2}} + (9.2 \\cdot 10^{9})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228269} {:answer/id #uuid "d4a028c9-87b3-5fdb-8ce0-e13f19c7179c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v^{2} = \\frac{2.3 \\cdot 10^{18}}{r} - (1.6 \\cdot 10^{7})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228270} {:answer/id #uuid "0c5266f5-54f8-5ad7-95da-336caafa30e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v^{2} = \\frac{1.15 \\cdot 10^{18}}{r} + (1.6 \\cdot 10^{7})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228271} {:answer/id #uuid "0eef3ee7-e041-535c-85dd-268c3df19f69" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v^{2} = \\frac{1.15 \\cdot 10^{18}}{r} - (9.2 \\cdot 10^{9})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228272}] :answer-field/id #uuid "f60acb06-e661-5395-b664-5819511bcf63" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228267} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1ef49603-a807-5f45-982c-373a15835b86" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{v}^{2}=\\frac{2.3\\times {10}^{18}}{r}-(1.6\\times {10}^{7})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391545} {:answer/id #uuid "041f118d-162c-5545-bb0c-a3cba2bcf150" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{v}^{2}=\\frac{1.15\\times {10}^{18}}{r}+(1.6\\times {10}^{7})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391546} {:answer/id #uuid "71fc6b2a-4a64-5b8d-aecb-dab45d68c6cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{v}^{2}=\\frac{2.3\\times {10}^{18}}{r}+(1.6\\times {10}^{7})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391547} {:answer/id #uuid "24e292a0-859a-569e-b479-5d6a968cab39" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{v}^{2}=\\frac{1.15\\times {10}^{18}}{r}-(9.2\\times {10}^{9})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391548} {:answer/id #uuid "639bf900-44e3-5ebc-86b0-d807c159046c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{v}^{2}=\\frac{2.3\\times {10}^{18}}{{r}^{2}}+(9.2\\times {10}^{9})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391549}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "71fc6b2a-4a64-5b8d-aecb-dab45d68c6cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{v}^{2}=\\frac{2.3\\times {10}^{18}}{r}+(1.6\\times {10}^{7})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391547} :answer-field/id #uuid "b31a8672-9812-5f3b-958f-3de2d774aebe" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391544}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "575cae6c-4604-5b3a-b514-5f09e281fdca" :question/math-academy-id "q-335240" :question/problem "Consider a spherical planet with radius $(2.5\\times {10}^{8})\\,\\text{m}$ and surface gravitational acceleration $g=18.4\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.$ Which equation best models the velocity $v$ of an object projected radially outward from the planet's surface with an initial velocity of $(9.6\\times {10}^{4})\\,\\text{m/s},$ where $r$ is the distance from the planet's center to the object. Assume the only force acting on the object is the force due to gravity." :question/worked-solution "Let $R$ be the planet's radius and $g$ be the planet's surface gravitational acceleration.\n\nAccording to Newton's law of universal gravitation, the magnitude of the inward gravitational force pulling the object toward the planet is\n\n$$\n{F}_{\\text{in}}=G\\,\\frac{mM}{{r}^{2}},\n$$\n\nwhere $G$ is the gravitational constant, $m$ is the object's mass, and $M$ is the planet's mass.\n\nSince this is the only force and it acts inward, the resultant force, taking *outward* as positive, is $F=-{F}_{\\text{in}}.$\nApplying Newton's second law, $F=ma,$ where $a$ is the acceleration of the object, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F & =-{F}_{\\text{in}} \\\\ ma & =-G\\,\\frac{mM}{{r}^{2}} \\\\ a & =-\\frac{GM}{{r}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn other words, the acceleration is inversely proportional to the square of the distance, \n\n$$\na=-\\frac{k}{{r}^{2}},\n$$\n\n with constant of proportionality $k=GM>0.$\n\nWhen at the surface, $r=R,$ the acceleration of the object due to gravity is $a=-g.$ Substituting into our previous equation, we can solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n-g=-\\frac{k}{{R}^{2}}\\,\\,\\,⟹\\,k=g{R}^{2}\n$$\n\nSubstituting back into the equation, we get\n\n$$\na=-\\frac{g{R}^{2}}{{r}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nNow, since acceleration $a$ is the derivative of velocity $v,$ which is the derivative of distance $r,$ we can express acceleration as follows using the chain rule, treating $v$ as a function of $r$ along the trajectory:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a & =\\frac{dv}{dt} \\\\  & =\\frac{dr}{dt}\\cdot \\frac{dv}{dr} \\\\  & =v\\frac{dv}{dr}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this result into our equation, we obtain a separable differential equation for the velocity:\n\n$$\nv\\frac{dv}{dr}=-\\frac{g{R}^{2}}{{r}^{2}}\n$$\n\nSolving using separation of variables, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int v\\frac{dv}{dr}\\,dr & =\\int (-\\frac{g{R}^{2}}{{r}^{2}})\\,dr \\\\ \\int v\\,dv & =-g{R}^{2}\\int \\frac{1}{{r}^{2}}\\,dr \\\\ \\frac{{v}^{2}}{2} & =-g{R}^{2}(-\\frac{1}{r})+{C}_{1} \\\\ {v}^{2} & =\\frac{2g{R}^{2}}{r}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $C=2{C}_{1}$ is a constant of integration.\n\nLet ${v}_{0}$ be the initial velocity of the object. Applying the initial condition $v(R)={v}_{0},$ we can solve for $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(R)^{2} & =\\frac{2g{R}^{2}}{R}+C \\\\ {v}_{0}^{2} & =2gR+C \\\\ C & ={v}_{0}^{2}-2gR\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation for the velocity of the object is\n\n$$\n{v}^{2}=\\frac{2g{R}^{2}}{r}+{v}_{0}^{2}-2gR.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting in the values\n$R=(2.5\\times {10}^{8}),$\n$g=18.4,$ and\n${v}_{0}=(9.6\\times {10}^{4}),$\nwe conclude that the equation for the velocity is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}^{2} & =\\frac{2\\cdot 18.4\\cdot (2.5\\times {10}^{8})^{2}}{r}+(9.6\\times {10}^{4})^{2}-2\\cdot 18.4\\cdot (2.5\\times {10}^{8}) \\\\  & =\\frac{230\\times {10}^{16}}{r}+(92.16\\times {10}^{8})-(92\\times {10}^{8}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2.3\\times {10}^{18}}{r}+((92.16-92)\\times {10}^{8}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2.3\\times {10}^{18}}{r}+(0.16\\times {10}^{8}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2.3\\times {10}^{18}}{r}+(1.6\\times {10}^{7}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229693 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1ac342bc-5d31-58a3-842a-8a4e79ae42dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "201.0\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377193} {:answer/id #uuid "2980a552-e3d5-5564-ab0e-1bbb2358ce8f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "204.3\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377194} {:answer/id #uuid "b4d44c02-122b-57ce-ae8c-3073cccbf6f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "207.6\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377195} {:answer/id #uuid "49a8983c-5113-540c-9960-edc50602eed7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "206.2\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377196} {:answer/id #uuid "8c1780f1-fe83-536d-9909-81d9b471da59" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "205.4\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377197}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b4d44c02-122b-57ce-ae8c-3073cccbf6f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "207.6\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377195} :answer-field/id #uuid "33da174c-337a-5186-96b2-62c3b16e65a3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377192}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "98f92dde-10ee-5a2e-ae0c-afe47e1981a5" :question/math-academy-id "q-336451" :question/problem "A de-icing salt reservoir can hold up to $300\\,\\text{gal}$ of liquid. Initially, it contains $180\\,\\text{gal}$ of brine with $54\\,\\text{lb}$ of dissolved salt. A stronger brine with concentration $0.8\\,\\text{lb/gal}$ flows into the reservoir at a rate of $6\\,\\text{gal/min},$ while the completely mixed brine drains out at a rate of $4\\,\\text{gal/min}.$\nFind the amount of salt in the reservoir at the moment it begins to overflow." :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "Since $Q$ is the amount of salt in the tank at time $t,$ the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t,$ in minutes. This is equal to the rate at which minerals enter the tank minus the rate at which minerals leave the tank.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{kg}/\\text{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which minerals enter the tank.\n\nFirst, note that a brine solution containing $0.8\\,\\text{lb/gal}$ of salt enters the reservoir at a rate of $6\\,\\text{gal/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *salt* enters the reservoir is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(0.8\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}})\\cdot (6\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(0.8\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}})\\cdot (6\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =4.8\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which minerals exit the tank.\n\nTo find the rate at which salt leaves the reservoir, we must first determine the volume of brine in the reservoir at time $t.$\n\nThe reservoir initially contains $180\\,\\text{gal}$ of brine; brine enters at $6\\,\\text{gal/min}$ and mixed brine leaves at $4\\,\\text{gal/min}.$\nAs a result, the amount of brine in the reservoir after $t$ minutes is \n\n$$\nV(t)=180+6t-4t=2(90+t)\\,\\text{gal}.\n$$\n\nNote that $V(t)$ is an increasing function, so the reservoir is filling.\n\nSince $Q$ is the amount of salt in the reservoir, the concentration of the brine at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{2(90+t)}\\,\\text{lb/gal}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed brine leaves at a rate of $4\\,\\text{gal/min},$ *salt* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(4\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{2(90+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}}) \\\\  & =(4\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{2(90+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{4Q}{2(90+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2Q}{90+t}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =4.8-\\frac{2Q}{90+t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{2}{90+t}\\,Q=4.8.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation.\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with variable coefficients. So we find the general solution using an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & ={e}^{\\int 2/(90+t)\\,dt} \\\\  & ={e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|90+t|} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(90+t)^{2}} \\\\  & =(90+t)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the differential equation by $I(t)=(90+t)^{2}$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(90+t)^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{2}{90+t}\\,Q) & =(90+t)^{2}\\cdot 4.8 \\\\ (90+t)^{2}\\frac{dQ}{dt}+2(90+t)Q & =4.8(90+t)^{2} \\\\ \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(90+t)^{2}) & =4.8(90+t)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(90+t)^{2})\\,dt & =\\int 4.8(90+t)^{2}\\,dt \\\\ Q(90+t)^{2} & =4.8\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}(90+t)^{3}+C \\\\ Q(t) & =1.6(90+t)+\\frac{C}{(90+t)^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that the reservoir initially contains $54\\,\\text{lb}$ of salt. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=54,$ and solve for $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =54 \\\\ 1.6(90+0)+\\frac{C}{(90+0)^{2}} & =54 \\\\ 144+\\frac{C}{(90)^{2}} & =54 \\\\ C & =-90(90)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(t) & =1.6(90+t)-\\frac{90(90)^{2}}{(90+t)^{2}} \\\\  & =1.6(90+t)-90{(\\frac{90}{90+t})}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let ${t}_{o}$ be the time when the reservoir begins to overflow. The reservoir has a capacity of $300\\,\\text{gal},$ so we can use the condition $V({t}_{o})=300$ to solve for ${t}_{o}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V({t}_{o}) & =300 \\\\ 2(90+{t}_{o}) & =300 \\\\ 90+{t}_{o} & =150 \\\\ {t}_{o} & =60\\,\\text{min}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the amount of salt in the reservoir at the moment it begins to overflow is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(60) & =1.6(90+60)-90{(\\frac{90}{90+60})}^{2} \\\\  & =240-90{(\\frac{9}{15})}^{2} \\\\  & =207.6\\,\\text{lb}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229698 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "132d6c9e-eb68-5d65-8d72-ee64a266345d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9.18\\,\\text{kg}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501500} {:answer/id #uuid "14f59116-2d5c-52e2-9b35-7f32ef8ca9aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8.21\\,\\text{kg}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501506} {:answer/id #uuid "23c67163-5885-5331-b6df-e6f1abd577ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9.81\\,\\text{kg}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501529} {:answer/id #uuid "df01cbb2-ff59-5cab-a987-8313d051432e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8.63\\,\\text{kg}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501913} {:answer/id #uuid "eb7cb287-8d09-5c98-9bf7-3d208192ac9a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7.99\\,\\text{kg}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501939}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "23c67163-5885-5331-b6df-e6f1abd577ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9.81\\,\\text{kg}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501529} :answer-field/id #uuid "4ad053d7-10e4-578d-b606-6bda94abf500" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228371}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "310f29d9-e5f9-5829-9c84-72d1324e1777" :question/math-academy-id "q-336738" :question/problem "A storage tank initially holds $600\\,\\text{L}$ of liquid containing $5\\,\\text{kg}$ of dissolved stabilizer.\nA feed solution containing $0.04\\,\\text{kg/L}$ of the stabilizer is pumped into the tank at a rate of $15\\,\\text{L/min},$ while the completely mixed liquid is drained out at a rate of $10\\,\\text{L/min}.$\nFind, rounded to two decimal places, the amount of stabilizer in the tank $10$ minutes after pumping begins." :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "Let $Q$ be the amount of stabilizer in the tank at time $t,$ in minutes. Then, the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t.$ This is equal to the rate at which stabilizer enters the tank minus the rate at which stabilizer leaves the tank.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{kg}/\\text{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which stabilizer enters the tank.\n\nNote that a feed solution containing $0.04\\,\\text{kg/L}$ of the stabilizer enters the tank at a rate of $15\\,\\text{L/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *stabilizer* enters the tank is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(0.04\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}})\\cdot (15\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(0.04\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}})\\cdot (15\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =0.6\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which stabilizer exits the tank.\n\nTo find the rate at which stabilizer leaves the tank, we must first determine the volume of liquid in the tank at time $t.$\n\nThe tank initially contains $600\\,\\text{L}$ of liquid; a solution enters at $15\\,\\text{L/min}$ and leaves at $10\\,\\text{L/min}.$\nAs a result, the volume of liquid in the tank after $t$ minutes is \n\n$$\n600+15t-10t=(600+5t)\\,\\text{L}.\n$$\n\nSince $Q$ is the amount of stabilizer in the tank, the *concentration* of the mixed solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{600+5t}\\,\\text{kg/L}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed liquid leaves at a rate of $10\\,\\text{L/min},$ *stabilizer* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(10\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{600+5t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}}) \\\\  & =(10\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{600+5t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{10Q}{600+5t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2Q}{120+t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =0.6-\\frac{2Q}{120+t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{2}{120+t}Q=0.6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation.\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with variable coefficients. So we find the general solution using an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & ={e}^{\\int 2/(120+t)\\,dt} \\\\  & ={e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|120+t|} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(120+t)^{2}} \\\\  & =(120+t)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the differential equation by $I(t)=(120+t)^{2}$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(120+t)^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{2}{120+t}Q) & =(120+t)^{2}\\cdot 0.6 \\\\ (120+t)^{2}\\frac{dQ}{dt}+2(120+t)Q & =0.6(120+t)^{2} \\\\ \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(120+t)^{2}) & =0.6(120+t)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(120+t)^{2})\\,dt & =\\int 0.6(120+t)^{2}\\,dt \\\\ Q(120+t)^{2} & =0.6\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}(120+t)^{3}+C \\\\ Q(t) & =0.2(120+t)+\\frac{C}{(120+t)^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $C$ is a constant of integration.\n\nWe're told that the tank initially contains $5\\,\\text{kg}$ of stabilizer. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=5,$ and solve for $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =5 \\\\ 0.2(120+0)+\\frac{C}{(120+0)^{2}} & =5 \\\\ 24+\\frac{C}{{120}^{2}} & =5 \\\\ \\frac{C}{{120}^{2}} & =-19 \\\\ C & =-19(120)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(t) & =0.2(120+t)-\\frac{19(120)^{2}}{(120+t)^{2}} \\\\  & =0.2(120+t)-19{(\\frac{120}{120+t})}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,  the amount of stabilizer in the tank $10$ minutes after pumping begins is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(10) & =0.2(120+10)-19{(\\frac{120}{120+10})}^{2} \\\\  & =26-19{(\\frac{12}{13})}^{2} \\\\  & ≈9.81\\,\\text{kg},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229699 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/id #uuid "aebdf2d3-26e8-577a-8477-2c2efb1a4064" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228377} {:answer-field/id #uuid "b4a42770-9433-54e8-b88d-ad4eac518c2e" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228378}] :question/id #uuid "a8534e01-c3a5-514f-aa2a-35a95571b90c" :question/math-academy-id "q-337100" :question/problem "Consider the d’Alembert differential equation\n$y = x(\\frac{dy}{dx})^{2} + \\frac{dy}{dx}$.\nUsing the substitution $t = \\frac{dy}{dx}$, the equation can be transformed to a first-order linear equation of the form\n$\\frac{dx}{dt} + P(t)x = Q(t)$.\nFind the functions $P$ and $Q$.$P(t) =$\n{{field-1}}\n$, Q(t) =$\n{{field-2}}"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229700 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0057fe33-994d-530f-9d6a-1d8d52f3e80d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(t)=1-2t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308744} {:answer/id #uuid "01633495-e9fe-50bd-a637-b4862253307f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(t)=2-2t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308753} {:answer/id #uuid "0c1110b5-8718-5257-8b4b-1114bbeec5af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(t)=2+t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308833} {:answer/id #uuid "e2427c75-b652-5799-8515-b39080a90533" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(t)=2+2t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310557} {:answer/id #uuid "ea4acb5e-4c58-59e0-96b1-5ed383eebc28" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(t)=2-t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310612}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "01633495-e9fe-50bd-a637-b4862253307f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(t)=2-2t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308753} :answer-field/id #uuid "c4b701e5-888a-5c98-92a7-f2756d373e8a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311359}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1782a6b5-a5c5-5e52-a9f2-0cebbb762ee2" :question/math-academy-id "q-337103" :question/problem "Consider the d’Alembert differential equation\n\n$$\ny=x(1+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}.\n$$\n\nIf ${y}^{′}(x)=t,$ then general solution can be expressed in parametric form $(x(t),y(t)),$ where\n\n$$\nx(t)=C{e}^{-t}+f(t)\n$$\n\nand $C$ is an arbitrary constant. Find the function $f(t).$\n\n*Hint: Under the given transformation, we have*\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+x=-2t.\n$$\n\n*You may also wish to make use of the following result (constants omitted):*\n\n$$\n\\int t{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t=(t-1){e}^{t}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "A differential equation is a d'Alembert differential equation if it can be expressed in the form\n\n$$\ny=xf({y}^{′})+g({y}^{′}),\n$$\n\nwhere $f$ and $g$ are differentiable functions of ${y}^{′}.$ \n\nWe're told that, under the substitution ${y}^{′}(x)=t,$ our d’Alembert differential equation transforms to the first-order linear equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+x=-2t.\n$$\n\nWe'll solve this equation using the method of integrating factors. Since this equation is already of the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+P(t)x=Q(t)\n$$\n\nwe compute our integrating factor as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & ={e}^{\\int P(t)\\,\\text{d}t}={e}^{\\int 1\\,\\text{d}t}={e}^{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying our differential equation by $I(t)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+x{e}^{t} & =-2t{e}^{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRewriting the left-hand side as an exact derivative, and integrating both sides of the equation with respect to $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(x{e}^{t}) & =-2t{e}^{t} \\\\ \\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(x{e}^{t})\\,\\text{d}t & =-2\\int t{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ x{e}^{t} & =-2\\int t{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ x{e}^{t} & =-2(t-1){e}^{t}+C \\\\ x & =-2(t-1)+C{e}^{-t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\nx(t)=C{e}^{-t}+2-2t\n$$\n\nand finally, $f(t)=2-2t.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229701 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "061efbf8-2b6a-5be5-bd7b-21ad06d93409" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{t}^{2}+t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308797} {:answer/id #uuid "21542faa-ec85-5250-b1f9-3ba21b860990" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{t}^{2}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309000} {:answer/id #uuid "b45ee349-9f71-5d3a-94ee-80ec66e795cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{t}^{2}-2t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310206} {:answer/id #uuid "d6ecaf40-a571-5685-8f52-3985bfa3e941" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{t}^{2}-t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310481} {:answer/id #uuid "fb390867-902b-58b1-a270-d17e8f675d73" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310753}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "061efbf8-2b6a-5be5-bd7b-21ad06d93409" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{t}^{2}+t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308797} :answer-field/id #uuid "5d2bf59d-6338-51c4-acd9-0887c0b311a9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311036}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b007f131-5b66-5321-9c2b-80d3724de4d7" :question/math-academy-id "q-337105" :question/problem "Consider the d’Alembert differential equation\n\n$$\ny=x{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}+(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}).\n$$\n\nIf ${y}^{′}(x)=t,$ then general solution can be expressed in parametric form $(x(t),y(t)),$ where\n\n$$\ny(t)=(\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡|t|-t+C}{(1-t)^{2}})f(t)+g(t)\n$$\n\nand $C$ is an arbitrary constant. Find $f(t)+g(t).$\n\n*Hint: Under the given transformation, we have*\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{2x}{t-1}=\\frac{1}{t(1-t)}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "A differential equation is a d'Alembert differential equation if it can be expressed in the form\n\n$$\ny=xf({y}^{′})+g({y}^{′}),\n$$\n\nwhere $f$ and $g$ are differentiable functions of ${y}^{′}.$ \n\nWe're told that, under the substitution ${y}^{′}(x)=t,$ our d’Alembert differential equation transforms to the first-order linear equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{2x}{t-1}=\\frac{1}{t(1-t)}\n$$\n\nWe'll solve this equation using the method of integrating factors. Since this equation is already of the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+P(t)x=Q(t)\n$$\n\nwe compute our integrating factor as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & ={e}^{\\int P(t)\\,\\text{d}t} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\int 2/(t-1)\\,\\text{d}t} \\\\  & ={e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|t-1|} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =(t-1)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying our differential equation by $I(t)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(t-1)^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+(t-1)^{2}\\frac{2x}{t-1}=(t-1)^{2}\\frac{1}{t(1-t)} \\\\ (t-1)^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+2(t-1)x=-(t-1)^{2}\\frac{1}{t(t-1)} \\\\ (t-1)^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+2(t-1)x=-\\frac{t-1}{t} \\\\ (t-1)^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+2(t-1)x=\\frac{1-t}{t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRewriting the left-hand side as an exact derivative, and integrating both sides of the equation with respect to $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(x(t-1)^{2}) & =\\frac{1-t}{t} \\\\ \\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(x(t-1)^{2})\\,\\text{d}t & =\\int \\frac{1-t}{t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ x(t-1)^{2} & =\\int \\frac{1}{t}-1\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ x(t-1)^{2} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|t|-t+C \\\\ x & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡|t|-t+C}{(t-1)^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\nx(t)=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡|t|-t+C}{(t-1)^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTo recover $y$, we substitute $x$ into\n\n$$\ny=x{t}^{2}+t,\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(t) & =(\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡|t|-t+C}{(t-1)^{2}}){t}^{2}+t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that $f(t)={t}^{2},g(t)=t,$ and\n\n$$\nf(t)+g(t)={t}^{2}+t.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229702 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a68a0bc6-f30d-5411-b064-805f776486bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228392} {:answer/id #uuid "bec5c4a2-e69a-58a3-94b3-3a681c2ec258" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228393} {:answer/id #uuid "3f269fd6-b9ae-5b17-b3d5-9362d69d8e71" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228394} {:answer/id #uuid "42cbcc13-0825-57af-be0a-c07b85f4c34d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228395} {:answer/id #uuid "67173ceb-8d72-5892-b1ac-cf5cbde74ac5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228396}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bec5c4a2-e69a-58a3-94b3-3a681c2ec258" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228393} :answer-field/id #uuid "d8042cd1-bac2-5d2b-b9a4-591acf3abc28" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228391}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d0d9a933-a0e8-56f2-b25b-1f97723df869" :question/math-academy-id "q-337169" :question/problem "![](images/a4/a4182416a97e5f0111b0823d6699f0ed39125f60289d7c7ebbb5f98d51d1a0f6.png)\n\nFind, in coulombs, the amplitude of the steady-state charge for the RC circuit shown above.\n\n*Hint: The voltage drop ${V}_{C}$ across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C},$ where $C$ is the capacitance measured in Farads.*" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "The diagram shows a series RC circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $20\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)\\,\\text{V},$ a resistor with resistance $20\\,Ω,$ and a capacitor with capacitance $\\frac{1}{60}\\,\\text{F}.$\n\nThe current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs. \n\n$$\nI=\\frac{\\text{d}q}{\\text{d}t}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RC circuit, there are two voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=20\\,Ω.$ So,\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=20I=20\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{V}_{C}=\\frac{q}{(\\frac{1}{60})}=60q.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ on the capacitor is\n\n$$\n20\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)=20\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+60q,\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{dq}{dt}+3q=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t).\n$$\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. So, to find the general solution, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${q}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{d{q}_{c}}{dt}+3{q}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{q}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-3t},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is sinusoidal with argument $4t.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also sinusoidal with argument $4t,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{q}_{p}(t)=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t).\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}_{p} & =\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t), \\\\ \\frac{d{q}_{p}}{dt} & =-4\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)+4\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ninto the inhomogeneous equation and group like terms on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-4\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)+4\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t))+3(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t) \\\\ (-4\\alpha +3\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)+(3\\alpha +4\\beta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-4\\alpha +3\\beta &=0\\, && (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)) \\\\ 3\\alpha +4\\beta &=1\\, && (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t))\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =\\frac{3}{25},\\beta =\\frac{4}{25}.$ Therefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{q}_{p}(t)=\\frac{3}{25}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+\\frac{4}{25}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t).\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nq(t)={q}_{c}(t)+{q}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-3t}+\\frac{3}{25}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+\\frac{4}{25}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t).\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The quantity ${q}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-3t}$ is the *transient charge*, since it goes to zero as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The function ${q}_{p}(t)=\\frac{3}{25}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+\\frac{4}{25}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)$ is the *steady-state charge*. It governs the charge's long-term behavior.\n\nTherefore, as $t\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\nq(t)≈{q}_{p}(t)=\\frac{3}{25}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+\\frac{4}{25}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t).\n$$\n\nFinally, the amplitude of the steady-state charge, in coulombs, is given by\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{{(\\frac{3}{25})}^{2}+{(\\frac{4}{25})}^{2}}=0.2.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229704 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/id #uuid "4dea68f2-1db4-5014-82eb-ddbb8d4d69e9" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228403} {:answer-field/id #uuid "61ef3c50-6638-5d76-a35c-7e1480d508b7" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228404}] :question/id #uuid "19be94de-0e17-54e2-85cf-71dbe2245d0d" :question/math-academy-id "q-337184" :question/problem "Consider the d’Alembert differential equation\n$y = x(\\frac{dy}{dx})^{3} + 2\\frac{dy}{dx}$.\nUsing the substitution $t = \\frac{dy}{dx}$, the equation can be transformed to a first-order linear equation of the form\n$\\frac{dx}{dt} + P(t)x = Q(t)$.\nFind the functions $P$ and $Q$.$P(t) =$\n{{field-1}}\n$, Q(t) =$\n{{field-2}}"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229711 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9325e5d1-fa4a-5ce0-80f1-2b1d73d21c24" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}\\cos 2x\\csc x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228440} {:answer/id #uuid "96716f40-d0f8-5848-8816-2a9ef9ed6bee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}\\cos 2x\\sec x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228441} {:answer/id #uuid "977267b3-794b-5090-b748-b772bdf84ab4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\cos 2x\\cot x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228442} {:answer/id #uuid "4d4e374c-61a5-55b6-af69-e44d8a4b2b3e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\cos 2x\\sec x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228443} {:answer/id #uuid "55f4c050-06c9-5796-a080-e7181bc8bb2a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\cos 2x\\csc x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228444}] :answer-field/id #uuid "81796914-d852-595b-8050-53c08ac44ecb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228439} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c7c4f0de-65d4-55a3-992e-a647b139e72a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\\operatorname{csc}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365833} {:answer/id #uuid "c4c65982-0650-5e0d-996a-e78f9bb5b6c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\\operatorname{csc}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365834} {:answer/id #uuid "12d444c7-9ae8-512f-98e6-830e37b66435" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\\operatorname{sec}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365835} {:answer/id #uuid "a9ab968b-1634-5de2-9e80-f73f99b4e935" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\\operatorname{cot}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365836} {:answer/id #uuid "56e33ae8-094c-5ec7-a04f-c7ad165cabe3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\\operatorname{sec}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365837}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "12d444c7-9ae8-512f-98e6-830e37b66435" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\\operatorname{sec}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365835} :answer-field/id #uuid "b9008acd-b16a-5420-b119-9306ceb1ba1d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365832}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c9674aca-ff32-53a9-8aab-6b30c0139952" :question/math-academy-id "q-337952" :question/problem "Consider the following differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+y={sec}^{3}⁡x.\n$$\n\nThe general solution to this equation can be written as\n\n$$\ny(x)=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+g(x),\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are constants. Which of the following could be the function $g(x)?$\n\n*Hint: Use the variation of parameters method. You may also wish to make use of the following result:*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x=1-2{sin}^{2}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have a second-order linear differential equation with linearly independent fundamental solutions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x).$ Then, according to the method of variation of parameters, we can seek a particular solution ${y}_{p}$ using the ansatz\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}(x) & ={u}_{1}(x){y}_{1}(x)+{u}_{2}(x){y}_{2}(x),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{1}$ and ${u}_{2}$ satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}^{′}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2}^{′} &=f(x) \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe solution to this system is\n\n$$\n{u}_{1}(x)=-\\int \\frac{f(x){y}_{2}(x)}{W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,{u}_{2}(x)=\\int \\frac{f(x){y}_{1}(x)}{W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})}\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nwhere $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})$ is the Wronskian.\n\nFirst, we note that the forcing term is $f(x)={sec}^{3}⁡x.$\n\nFrom the given information, we see that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}(x)=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the fundamental solutions to the corresponding homogeneous equation are\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,\\,{y}_{2}(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\n$$\n\nWe can use the method of variation of parameters if these solutions are independent. So, let's first compute the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}-{y}_{1}^{′}{y}_{2} \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)^{′}-(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)^{′}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)-(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\  & ={cos}^{2}⁡x+{sin}^{2}⁡x \\\\  & =1\\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the Wronskian is not equal to zero, this confirms that the functions  ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are independent. \n\nLet's compute ${u}_{1}(x)$ and ${u}_{2}(x):$\n\n- First, we compute ${u}_{1}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}(x) & =-\\int \\frac{f(x){y}_{2}(x)}{W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\int \\frac{{sec}^{3}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡x{sec}^{3}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\int \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{{cos}^{3}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $u=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$ Then, we have $\\text{d}u=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x,$ and our integral becomes\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\int \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{{cos}^{3}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{{u}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2{u}^{2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2{cos}^{2}⁡x} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}{sec}^{2}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Then, we compute ${u}_{2}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{2}(x) & =\\int \\frac{f(x){y}_{1}(x)}{W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{{sec}^{3}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡x{sec}^{3}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{{cos}^{3}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{{cos}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int {sec}^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}(x) & ={u}_{1}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}{y}_{2} \\\\  & =(-\\frac{1}{2}{sec}^{2}⁡x)(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)+(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\  & =(-\\frac{1}{2{cos}^{2}⁡x})\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x})\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}+\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} \\\\  & =\\frac{-1+2{sin}^{2}⁡x}{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1-2{sin}^{2}⁡x}{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x}{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\\operatorname{sec}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe general solution is the sum of the complementary and particular solutions, and can be written as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(x) & ={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x) \\\\  & =A\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\underbrace{\\,-\\,\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\\operatorname{sec}⁡x}_{g(x)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\ng(x)=-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\\operatorname{sec}⁡x.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229735 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "85f8cae8-00e4-5d66-b7ab-8dc7d80eb1b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "s \\ge - 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228635} {:answer/id #uuid "aa6f841c-04c9-5850-890f-aa152557ae6f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "s > 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228636} {:answer/id #uuid "c10defcd-985c-5215-b98f-597ae2b2aa5e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "s \\ge 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228637} {:answer/id #uuid "c71bac8d-4f40-5ed8-825d-3d0b67c9b073" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "s > - 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228638} {:answer/id #uuid "10ffff16-3406-501c-9ce5-7755d50b3fa6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "s > 3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228639} {:answer/id #uuid "39b1a55a-38c1-51b6-aba1-d6079a76bd16" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "s \\ge 3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228640}] :answer-field/id #uuid "778ec954-d39f-5e6d-8488-6b309a892a0e" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228634} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "adccceeb-d32f-59db-aa1e-92b62269b48b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(e^{3(2 + s)})/(s + 2) + \\frac{e^{-3s}}{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228642} {:answer/id #uuid "cef7de00-c987-5b00-a44e-78f2a653c4cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(e^{-3(2 + s)})/(s + 2) + \\frac{e^{-3s}}{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228643} {:answer/id #uuid "f946910f-e6ec-51c0-a423-f8a981ca9df9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1 - e^{-3(2 + s)})/(2 + s) + \\frac{e^{-3s}}{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228644} {:answer/id #uuid "42b1a7e2-7693-51e2-a44e-f9d53497b46c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(e^{-3(2 + s)} - 1)/(s + 2) - \\frac{e^{-3s}}{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228645}] :answer-field/id #uuid "db594378-e8f7-5fa2-a95a-87516b9ff8a3" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228641} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9c4c533f-908a-567e-bec5-a34f0fb4c795" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "s\\ge -2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368304} {:answer/id #uuid "e33815ee-25fe-5ccb-b369-f0725f4674b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "s>-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368305} {:answer/id #uuid "a5ad58bc-98b4-5f21-a938-56897b11dd98" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "s>3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368306} {:answer/id #uuid "93b4c7cd-4771-5107-8cfd-b99977ef7671" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "s\\ge 3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368307} {:answer/id #uuid "844e6b9d-a130-5b9a-a07c-6bc9ae2eab75" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "s\\ge 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368308} {:answer/id #uuid "d21f58f8-5853-5eb6-84b6-008b143b0b84" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "s>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368309}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d21f58f8-5853-5eb6-84b6-008b143b0b84" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "s>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368309} :answer-field/id #uuid "26c0fa7b-08b6-5bc4-a9f0-3be3ef51d6f1" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368303} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7da22ad8-b712-52ee-a8cd-593d4497e34a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{3(2+s)}}{s+2}+\\frac{{e}^{-3s}}{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368311} {:answer/id #uuid "a0d999a6-3413-502a-947d-25b79356a95c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{-3(2+s)}}{s+2}+\\frac{{e}^{-3s}}{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368312} {:answer/id #uuid "1be7bc5f-1c6d-5d9e-a223-91b8ee41e59e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{-3(2+s)}-1}{s+2}-\\frac{{e}^{-3s}}{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368313} {:answer/id #uuid "f63fe085-f93d-5fcd-bf37-289a3afe47a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1-{e}^{-3(2+s)}}{2+s}+\\frac{{e}^{-3s}}{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368314}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f63fe085-f93d-5fcd-bf37-289a3afe47a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1-{e}^{-3(2+s)}}{2+s}+\\frac{{e}^{-3s}}{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368314} :answer-field/id #uuid "a9132969-223c-5250-b50b-37fcd34fa6cd" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368310}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e403a919-07cd-5914-ae97-646903c631b6" :question/math-academy-id "q-341480" :question/problem "Use the definition of Laplace transforms to calculate $F(s)=L{f(t)},$ where $f(t)$ is given by \n\n$$\nf(t)={\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-2t},\\, & t\\le 3 \\\\ 1\\, & t>3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$\\,F(s)=L{f(t)}=$ {{field-1}},\nvalid for {{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "We apply the definition of the Laplace transform:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{f(t)} & =F(s)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }f(t){e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, for the piecewise function \n\n$$\nf(t)={\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-2t},\\, & t\\le 3 \\\\ 1\\, & t>3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }f(t){e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{e}^{-2t}\\cdot {e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{3}^{\\infty }1\\cdot {e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{e}^{-2t-st}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{3}^{\\infty }1\\cdot {e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{e}^{-(2+s)t}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{3}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that the improper integral diverges if $s\\le 0.$\n\nFor $s>0,$ we calculate our Laplace transform as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{e}^{-(2+s)t}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{3}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{-(2+s)}{[{e}^{-(2+s)t}]}_{0}^{3}+{\\int }_{3}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1-{e}^{-3(2+s)}}{2+s}+{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{3}^{a}{e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1-{e}^{-3(2+s)}}{2+s}+{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{(\\frac{-{e}^{-st}}{s})|}_{3}^{a} \\\\  & =\\frac{1-{e}^{-3(2+s)}}{2+s}-\\frac{1}{s}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }({e}^{-as}-{e}^{-3s}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1-{e}^{-3(2+s)}}{2+s}-\\frac{1}{s}\\cdot (0-{e}^{-3s}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1-{e}^{-3(2+s)}}{2+s}+\\frac{{e}^{-3s}}{s}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nvalid for $s>0.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229742 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c2e9c02f-14e9-5f32-b79c-a293214a34bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y_{2}(x) = y_{1}(x)\\ln x + x^{-1/2}∑_(n = 0)^(∞)a_{n}(-\\frac{1}{2})x^{n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228673} {:answer/id #uuid "d5208b18-0f8d-53aa-a1d9-e511b152b957" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y_{2}(x) = b_{-1}y_{1}(x)\\ln x + x^{-1/2}∑_(n = 0)^(∞)a_{n}(-\\frac{1}{2})x^{n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228674} {:answer/id #uuid "4413815d-7aad-58c0-b7fa-87fe8642d713" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y_{2}(x) = y_{1}(x)\\ln x + x^{3}∑_(n = 0)^(∞)a_{n}(3)x^{n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228675} {:answer/id #uuid "48ee413e-53fb-5a1f-8135-e79ae7b4b460" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y_{2}(x) = x^{-1/2}∑_(n = 0)^(∞)a_{n}(-\\frac{1}{2})x^{n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228676} {:answer/id #uuid "55c8cb48-ff13-5ae2-8cde-4c339e88fd7b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y_{2}(x) = x^{3}∑_(n = 0)^(∞)a_{n}(3)x^{n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228677}] :answer-field/id #uuid "297f2c43-91f3-5db3-a212-9faef1b723df" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228672} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e5631038-02ff-5478-959b-702050aff1b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{2}(x)={x}^{3}{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}(3){x}^{n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379642} {:answer/id #uuid "2f485562-fee4-5334-bd58-776a6dc64a79" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{2}(x)={b}_{-1}{y}_{1}(x)\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+{x}^{-1/2}{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\,(-\\frac{1}{2}){x}^{n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379643} {:answer/id #uuid "ae1ae533-0b8b-5428-bafe-f583f9b82f26" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{2}(x)={y}_{1}(x)\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+{x}^{-1/2}{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\,(-\\frac{1}{2}){x}^{n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379644} {:answer/id #uuid "97172b7b-d96a-5752-bf3b-37b017cabfc1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{2}(x)={x}^{-1/2}{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\,(-\\frac{1}{2}){x}^{n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379645} {:answer/id #uuid "7881ac04-9b8a-5136-a88c-49ff54bf4f9e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{2}(x)={y}_{1}(x)\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+{x}^{3}{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}(3){x}^{n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379646}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "97172b7b-d96a-5752-bf3b-37b017cabfc1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{2}(x)={x}^{-1/2}{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\,(-\\frac{1}{2}){x}^{n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379645} :answer-field/id #uuid "d405bd5b-5125-52fd-8365-832ff3fb0d15" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379641}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3d16c3fa-9c4f-5cce-a9e5-386a0c61403b" :question/math-academy-id "q-342494" :question/problem "Consider the following differential equation.\n\n$$\n2{x}^{2}{y}^{″}+x(2x-3){y}^{′}-3y=0\n$$\n\nThe general solution near $x=0$ of the differential equation above is given by\n\n$$\ny={c}_{1}{x}^{3}{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}(3){x}^{n}+{c}_{2}{y}_{2}(x),\n$$\n\nwhere ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$ are arbitrary constants and ${a}_{n}(\\lambda )$ are coefficients dependent on $\\lambda $ (solutions of the indicial equation) and determined by substitution.\nWhich of the following is the form of the second Frobenius solution ${y}_{2}?$\n\n*Hint: You may assume $x=0$ is a regular singular point of the ODE and the indicial equation of the ODE is $2{\\lambda }^{2}-5\\lambda -3=0.$*" :question/worked-solution "The general solution near a regular singular point $x=0$ of a second-order homogeneous linear differential equation with indicial roots ${\\lambda }_{1}\\ge {\\lambda }_{2}$ is given by\n\n$$\ny={c}_{1}{y}_{1}(x)+{c}_{2}{y}_{2}(x),\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)={x}^{{\\lambda }_{1}}{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}({\\lambda }_{1}){x}^{n}\n$$\n\nis the first Frobenius solution, and the second Frobenius solution is determined as follows:\n\n- **Case 1**: If ${\\lambda }_{1}-{\\lambda }_{2}\\notin Z,$ then \n${y}_{2}(x)={x}^{{\\lambda }_{2}}{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}({\\lambda }_{2}){x}^{n}.$\n\n- **Case 2**: If ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\lambda }_{2},$ then\n${y}_{2}(x)={y}_{1}(x)\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+{x}^{{\\lambda }_{1}}{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}{x}^{n}.$\n\n- **Case 3**: If ${\\lambda }_{1}-{\\lambda }_{2}\\in N,$ then\n${y}_{2}(x)={b}_{-1}{y}_{1}(x)\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+{x}^{{\\lambda }_{2}}{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}{x}^{n}.$\n\nWe are given that the indicial equation is $2{\\lambda }^{2}-5\\lambda -3=0.$\nSolving for the roots, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{\\lambda }^{2}-5\\lambda -3 & =0 \\\\ (2\\lambda +1)(\\lambda -3) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =-\\frac{1}{2},3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe larger solution is ${\\lambda }_{1}=3\\ge -\\frac{1}{2}={\\lambda }_{2}.$ Therefore, the first Frobenius series solution we take is\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)={x}^{3}{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}(3){x}^{n}.\n$$\n\nNow, since ${\\lambda }_{1}-{\\lambda }_{2}=3-(-\\frac{1}{2})=\\frac{7}{2}$ *is not* an integer, the other solution is the second Frobenius solution, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(x)={x}^{-1/2}{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\,(-\\frac{1}{2}){x}^{n}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231320 :question/id #uuid "dc812edf-4737-575a-937b-89bc2af88c38" :question/math-academy-id "e-21963" :question/problem "If $r=\\dfrac57$, then $r^2=$" :question/worked-solution "Replacing $r$ with\n\n$$\n\\dfrac57\n$$\n\nin the expression, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r^{2} &= (\\frac{5}{7})^{2} \\\\ &= \\frac{5}{7} \\cdot \\frac{5}{7} \\\\ &= \\frac{5 \\cdot 5}{7 \\cdot 7} \\\\ &= \\frac{25}{49} \\mid\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231326 :question/id #uuid "8df55194-b24b-54ad-b6cb-1ec76c833210" :question/math-academy-id "e-21970" :question/problem "The amount of paint (in liters) used to cover a wall is given by the expression $\\dfrac12w^2+\\dfrac34$, where $w$ is the width of the wall in meters. How much paint is used when the width of the wall is $\\dfrac54\\,\\textrm{m}$?" :question/worked-solution "To determine how much paint is used when the width of the wall is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac54\\,\\textrm{m}\n$$\n\nwe evaluate the expression at\n\n$$\nw=\\dfrac54\n$$\n\nFirst, replacing $w$ with\n\n$$\n\\dfrac54\n$$\n\nin the expression, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}w^{2} + \\frac{3}{4} &= \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot w^{2} + \\frac{3}{4} \\\\ &= \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot (\\frac{5}{4})^{2} + \\frac{3}{4} \\\\ &= \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot \\frac{25}{16} + \\frac{3}{4} \\mid\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, evaluating the resulting expression, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} \\cdot \\frac{25}{16} + \\frac{3}{4} &= \\frac{25}{32} + \\frac{3}{4} \\\\ &= \\frac{25}{32} + \\frac{24}{32} \\\\ &= \\frac{49}{32} \\mid\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the amount of paint used is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{49}{32}\\,\\textrm{L}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231572 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1fb9ae0a-ccab-5851-bc46-d8fb84f0673a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537298} {:answer/id #uuid "391bcc0e-c46d-58de-a271-e5921f5359be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537346} {:answer/id #uuid "3cf55ac9-9213-51d1-bb90-ad44b88e7647" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537354} {:answer/id #uuid "93cc08d4-e51b-59b2-b080-974e07e72850" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537506}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1fb9ae0a-ccab-5851-bc46-d8fb84f0673a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537298} :answer-field/id #uuid "23b2f25e-b1a1-5a98-851d-8c3126524b1c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186230302}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0c02a55f-b3d6-55fc-b2ef-8b484c0659e0" :question/math-academy-id "q-343663" :question/problem "Consider the boundary value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+3y=f(x)\n$$\n\non $[-2,2]$, where $f$ is $4$-periodic and odd. Which Fourier-series form is appropriate for a particular solution ${y}_{p}?$\n\n$\\,$\n${y}_{p}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Because $f$ is $4$-periodic on $[-2,2]$, we have $L=2$, so Fourier series on this interval use the basis \n\n$$\ncos\\,(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2})\\,\\text{and}\\,sin\\,(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}).\n$$\n\nNext, since $f$ is odd, its Fourier series contains only sine terms. For a second-order equation with no ${y}^{′}$ (damping) term, the particular solution inherits this property and is also an odd function.\n\nTherefore, the appropriate form for ${y}_{p}$ is a sine series:\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2})\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231575 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "08e980a7-73e5-5db5-b117-dad8065a2ce3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{6})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508401} {:answer/id #uuid "7899c111-a756-5f84-bb7b-8a3a2fc1f66f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{6})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508565} {:answer/id #uuid "846bcad3-1671-5fb8-b71c-e4babf98109d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508588} {:answer/id #uuid "aff8259e-8a40-5b92-873a-8c1bbf4d08f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508656}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "846bcad3-1671-5fb8-b71c-e4babf98109d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508588} :answer-field/id #uuid "820b3701-ddd6-58d9-9b7a-77aa5711efbd" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186230317}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3ccc73a6-d864-55e4-9da3-f78dd465c648" :question/math-academy-id "q-343671" :question/problem "Consider the boundary value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+3y=f(x)\n$$\n\non $[-3,3]$, where $f$ is $6$-periodic and even. Which Fourier-series form is appropriate for a particular solution ${y}_{p}?$\n\n$\\,$\n${y}_{p}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Because $f$ is $6$-periodic on $[-3,3]$, we have $L=3$, so Fourier series on this interval use the basis\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})\\,\\text{and}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}).\n$$\n\nNext, since $f$ is even, its Fourier series contains only cosine terms. For a second-order equation with no ${y}^{′}$ (damping) term, the particular solution inherits this property and is also an even function.\n\nTherefore, the appropriate form for ${y}_{p}$ is a cosine series:\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231585 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b1bb9f4b-6c2e-5d6b-87d0-78e4c9a5b1f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{33}{50}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186230377} {:answer/id #uuid "c2d3d421-418f-598d-b619-f761de70afeb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186230378} {:answer/id #uuid "353a8f78-ce46-5d98-86a4-c93a4fd5aa63" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186230379} {:answer/id #uuid "3c9ff9c8-f28b-5bbc-b56a-9dca8a667374" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186230380} {:answer/id #uuid "3d8c51ea-ffd9-5c44-a039-8d1f95f96d8d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186230381}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3d8c51ea-ffd9-5c44-a039-8d1f95f96d8d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186230381} :answer-field/id #uuid "00eff77d-799b-55ba-877d-1720b58cb296" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186230376}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f4b2630f-dea5-5b20-90c5-3365567316a5" :question/math-academy-id "q-344172" :question/problem "![](images/0d/0d8f6711f76fa9e55f11087c99d0e626ebc665e0f44beeefe3f67e9d35159618.png)\n\nFind, in amperes, the amplitude of the steady state current for the RCL circuit shown above.\n\n*Hint: The voltage drop ${V}_{C}$ across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C},$ where $C$ is the capacitance measured in Farads.*" :question/worked-solution "The diagram shows a series RCL circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $5\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)\\,\\text{V},$ a resistor of resistance $10\\,Ω,$ a capacitor of capacitance $0.2\\,\\text{F},$ and an inductor of inductance $5\\,\\text{H}.$\n\nThe current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs.\n\n$$\nI=\\frac{dq}{dt}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RCL circuit, there are three voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=10\\,Ω.$ So,\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=10I.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{V}_{C}=\\frac{q}{C}=\\frac{q}{0.2}=5q.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=5\\,\\text{H}.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n{V}_{L}=5\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, these values are related by the equation\n\n$$\n5\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)=10I+5q+5\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating with respect to $t,$ the differential equation governing the current in the circuit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{d}{dt}(5\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)) & =\\frac{d}{dt}(10I+5q+5\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}) \\\\ -15\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) & =10\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}+5\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+5\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}I}{d{t}^{2}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich, when substituting in $I=\\frac{dq}{dt},$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{{d}^{2}I}{d{t}^{2}}+2\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}+I=-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nThe given equation is a second-order linear equation with constant coefficients. To find the general solution, we must sum the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${I}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{d}^{2}{I}_{c}}{d{t}^{2}}+2\\,\\frac{d{I}_{c}}{dt}+{I}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as a trial solution, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{I}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-t}+Bt{e}^{-t},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are constants.\n\n- Next, we find a particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is sinusoidal with argument $3t.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also sinusoidal with argument $3t,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{I}_{p}(t)=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute this, as well as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{d{I}_{p}}{dt} & =-3\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)+3\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t), \\\\ \\frac{{d}^{2}{I}_{p}}{d{t}^{2}} & =-9\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-9\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ninto the inhomogeneous equation and group like terms on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-9\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-9\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t))+2(-3\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)+3\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)) \\\\ +(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)) & =-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) \\\\ (-8\\alpha +6\\beta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+(-6\\alpha -8\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) & =-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-8\\alpha +6\\beta &=0\\, && (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)) \\\\ -6\\alpha -8\\beta &=-3\\, && (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t))\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =\\frac{9}{50}$ and $\\beta =\\frac{6}{25}.$ Therefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{I}_{p}(t)=\\frac{9}{50}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\frac{6}{25}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nI(t)={I}_{c}(t)+{I}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-t}+Bt{e}^{-t}+\\frac{9}{50}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\frac{6}{25}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The quantity ${I}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-t}+Bt{e}^{-t}$ is the *transient current*, since it goes to zero as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The function ${I}_{p}(t)=\\frac{9}{50}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\frac{6}{25}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)$ is the *steady-state current*. It governs the current's long-term behavior.\n\nTherefore, as $t\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\nI(t)≈{I}_{p}(t)=\\frac{9}{50}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\frac{6}{25}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nFinally, the amplitude of the steady-state current, in amperes, is given by\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{{(\\frac{9}{50})}^{2}+{(\\frac{6}{25})}^{2}}=\\frac{3}{10}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233030 :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "62c0b89d-1d8a-5723-8092-97486825267c" :question/math-academy-id "q-100459" :question/problem "If ${216}^{3a+4}={(\\frac{1}{36})}^{6a-2}$ then $a=$" :question/worked-solution "To solve this equation, we need both exponential terms to have a common base. To do this, we rewrite the equation using $216={6}^{3}$ and $36={6}^{2},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{({6}^{3})}^{3a+4}={(\\frac{1}{{6}^{2}})}^{6a-2}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the power rule and the quotient rule of exponents. This gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{6}^{3(3a+4)}=\\frac{1}{{6}^{2(6a-2)}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we rewrite the right-hand side using a negative exponent:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{6}^{3(3a+4)}={6}^{-2(6a-2)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since both sides of the equation share the same base $(6)$, we equate the exponents and solve for $a:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3(3a+4) & =-2(6a-2) \\\\ 9a+12 & =-12a+4 \\\\ 21a & =-8 \\\\ a & =-\\frac{8}{21}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233051 :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d746064f-9bc3-5f26-9c93-afaf01c3e7e9" :question/math-academy-id "q-101719" :question/problem "Given that $y=7-4{log}_{3}x$, find the instantaneous rate of change at $x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}3}.$" :question/worked-solution "To differentiate a logarithm with base $a,$ we use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({log}_{a}x)=\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}a}.\n$$\n\nTo find the instantaneous rate of change at a given point, we have to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ at that point.\n\nTaking the derivative we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(7-4{log}_{3}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(7)-4\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({log}_{3}x) \\\\  & =0-4\\cdot \\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}3} \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{x\\operatorname{ln}3}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the instantaneous rate of change at $x=1/\\operatorname{ln}3$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=1/\\operatorname{ln}3} & ={(-\\frac{4}{x\\operatorname{ln}3})\\,|}_{x=1/\\operatorname{ln}3} \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{1} \\\\  & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233067 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "10447d27-bbad-5243-827a-0ec88f52db0b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{′}(3)=48" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512131} {:answer/id #uuid "6db8fd27-a6bb-5d05-8c7a-556b2db63a39" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{′}(3)=60" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512359} {:answer/id #uuid "71a944ae-ff58-5447-8134-5585f04d64b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{′}(3)=30" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512370} {:answer/id #uuid "b98cc99c-1897-5580-9a45-71a60760d8c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{′}(3)=40" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512546} {:answer/id #uuid "d6da7332-d0ae-5c57-a840-c9de6058e7d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{′}(3)=72" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512610}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6db8fd27-a6bb-5d05-8c7a-556b2db63a39" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{′}(3)=60" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512359} :answer-field/id #uuid "1bf2afe7-a005-5071-84b9-bd40024ce506" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512738}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0d862336-2821-5236-aa0c-5f338ff1395d" :question/math-academy-id "q-102613" :question/problem "Find the instantaneous rate of change of $f(x)=10{x}^{2}$ at the point $x=3$ by computing the limit of a difference quotient." :question/worked-solution "*Method 1:*\n\nBy the definition, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(3)={lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{f(3+h)-f(3)}{h}.\n$$\n\nApplying this to $f(x)=10{x}^{2}$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(3) & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}(\\frac{10(3+h)^{2}-10(3)^{2}}{h}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}(\\frac{90+60h+10{h}^{2}-90}{h}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}(\\frac{60h+10{h}^{2}}{h}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}(60+10h) \\\\  & =60+(0) \\\\  & =60.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\n*Method 2:*\n\nBy the definition, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(3)={lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{f(x)-f(3)}{x-3}.\n$$\n\nApplying this to $f(x)=10{x}^{2}$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(3) & ={lim}_{x\\to 3}(\\frac{10{x}^{2}-10(3)^{2}}{x-3}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 3}(\\frac{10({x}^{2}-{3}^{2})}{x-3}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 3}(\\frac{10(x-3)(x+3)}{x-3}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 3}10(x+3) \\\\  & =10(3+3) \\\\  & =60.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233077 :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "cba81123-55c3-55a5-9eb0-35c472e805bb" :question/math-academy-id "q-103257" :question/problem "Consider the sequence starting from ${a}_{1}=\\frac{6}{5}$  with the property $\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}=\\frac{3}{4}$ for all $n\\ge 1.$ Write the sum to infinity of the terms of this sequence using sigma notation." :question/worked-solution "Notice that the sequence is a geometric sequence since the ratio of any two successive terms is constant.\n\nThe first term of the sequence is ${a}_{1}=\\frac{6}{5},$ and the common ratio is\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}=\\frac{3}{4}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general term ${a}_{n}$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}{(r)}^{n-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{5}{(\\frac{3}{4})}^{n-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{5}{(\\frac{3}{4})}^{-1}{(\\frac{3}{4})}^{n} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{5}{(\\frac{3}{4})}^{n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the infinite geometric series can be written in sigma notation as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n} & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }(\\frac{8}{5}){(\\frac{3}{4})}^{n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233087 :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8cc9778d-34a0-53fe-a02e-327d24fefea1" :question/math-academy-id "q-104437" :question/problem "Find the slope of $y=f(x)$ at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ if $f(x)=x\\operatorname{cot}x.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we take the derivative using the product rule:\n\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x\\operatorname{cot}x) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cot}x+x(-{csc}^{2}x) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cot}x-x{csc}^{2}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute $x=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ in the derivative and get\n\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}{|}_{\\pi /4} & =\\operatorname{cot}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})-\\frac{\\pi }{4}{csc}^{2}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =1-\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\cdot (\\sqrt{2})^{2} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233101 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "37c08502-d0d5-5e72-af2d-a3ef7cdf0292" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=2\\sqrt{3}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241675} {:answer/id #uuid "4bf2067a-14ff-5a00-adc0-d41c69dcd714" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=\\sqrt{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{4})+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241861} {:answer/id #uuid "659f5ff8-46d6-55a7-a779-874f201a7a8f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=3\\sqrt{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{4})+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242093} {:answer/id #uuid "f5df124e-f5dc-548a-9350-06239c8666e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=\\sqrt{3}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{4})+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{4}))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243353} {:answer/id #uuid "ffc7784a-720a-585a-a0f1-5b62e36cba32" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=3\\sqrt{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{4})+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{4}))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243432}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ffc7784a-720a-585a-a0f1-5b62e36cba32" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=3\\sqrt{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{4})+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{4}))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243432} :answer-field/id #uuid "84916584-efb6-58b6-a99a-7f67a30e54da" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243690}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4c02d420-5776-5e40-b2a8-716b6f6c3925" :question/math-academy-id "q-107499" :question/problem "Express $z=\\frac{6}{-1+\\text{i}}$ in the form $r(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we express the given complex number in the form $z=x+y\\text{i}.$ To do this, we remove $\\text{i}$ from the denominator by multiplying the numerator and denominator of $z$ by the complex conjugate of the denominator, which is $-1-\\text{i}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =\\frac{6}{-1+\\text{i}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6(-1-\\text{i})}{(-1+\\text{i})(-1-\\text{i})} \\\\  & =\\frac{6(-1-\\text{i})}{(-1)^{2}-{\\text{i}}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6(-1-\\text{i})}{1+1} \\\\  & =3(-1-\\text{i}) \\\\  & =-3-3\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nIn polar form, $r$ represents the magnitude $|z|.$ For a complex number $z=x+y\\text{i},$ the magnitude of $z$ is given by\n\n$$\n|z|=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nSo, for $z=-3-3\\text{i},$ the magnitude is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=|z| & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-3)^{2}+(-3)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{18} \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n    \n\nIn polar form, $\\theta $  represents the argument $\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z).$ Since $x=-3$ and $y=-3$ are both negative, $z$ lies in the third quadrant. So, we can find $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta =\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z) & =\\pi +\\operatorname{arctan}⁡|\\frac{y}{x}| \\\\  & =\\pi +\\operatorname{arctan}⁡|\\frac{-3}{-3}| \\\\  & =\\pi +\\operatorname{arctan}⁡1 \\\\  & =\\pi +\\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\pi }{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the complex number $z$ in polar form:\n\n$$\nz=3\\sqrt{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{4})+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{4}))\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233107 :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "8dfbbbdb-5ea1-54bb-aa69-852fdb1bfb31" :question/math-academy-id "q-107659" :question/problem "Express the following arithmetic series using sigma notation.\n\n$$\n2+\\frac{1}{4}-\\frac{3}{2}+…-\\frac{41}{4}-12\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The first term ${a}_{1}=2$ and the common difference is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & ={a}_{2}-{a}_{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}-2 \\\\  & =-\\frac{7}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the formula for the $n$th term is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}+(n-1)d \\\\  & =2+(n-1)(-\\frac{7}{4}) \\\\  & =2-\\frac{7}{4}n+\\frac{7}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{15}{4}-\\frac{7}{4}n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe don't know the index of the last term, but we can find it using the formula for the $n$th term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & =-12 \\\\ \\frac{15}{4}-\\frac{7}{4}n & =-12 \\\\ \\frac{7}{4}n & =\\frac{15}{4}+12 \\\\ \\frac{7}{4}n & =\\frac{63}{4} \\\\ n & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThere are $9$ terms in total, from $n=1$ to $n=9.$ So, the sigma notation for the given sum is:\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{9}(\\frac{15}{4}-\\frac{7}{4}n)\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233113 :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "49708de8-941e-50f5-938e-ceca5ba94528" :question/math-academy-id "q-107794" :question/problem "Calculate the sum of the first $15$ terms of the following arithmetic series.\n\n$$\n-1+(-\\frac{2}{5})+\\frac{1}{5}+\\frac{4}{5}+⋯\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To calculate the sum of an arithmetic series, we use the formula\n\n$$\n{S}_{N}=\\frac{N}{2}(2{a}_{1}+(N-1)d),\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{1}$ is the first term, $d$ is the common difference, and $N$ is the number of terms.\n\nWe are given the total number of terms $N=15$ and the first term is ${a}_{1}=-1.$ Now, the common difference $d$ can be found as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & ={a}_{2}-{a}_{1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{5}-(-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${a}_{1}=-1$, $d=\\frac{3}{5}$, and $N=15$ into the above formula for ${S}_{N}$, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{15} & =\\frac{N}{2}(2{a}_{1}+(N-1)d) \\\\  & =\\frac{15}{2}(2(-1)+(15-1)\\cdot \\frac{3}{5}) \\\\  & =\\frac{15}{2}\\cdot (-2+8.4) \\\\  & =7.5\\cdot 6.4 \\\\  & =48\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233116 :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e3220f14-15f6-53f4-bf67-85844b3d99e0" :question/math-academy-id "q-108505" :question/problem "Calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ for ${x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}(y)=8{x}^{3}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we differentiate both sides of the equation with respect to $x,$ assuming that $y=y(x).$\n\nNote that to differentiate the ${x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}(y)$ term, we will need to use the product rule.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}(y) & =8{x}^{3} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}(y)) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(8{x}^{3}) \\\\ [({x}^{2})(\\frac{1}{y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{dy}}{\\text{d}x})+(2x)(\\operatorname{ln}(y))] & =24{x}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we solve the equation for $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{y}(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})+2x\\operatorname{ln}(y) & =24{x}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{y}(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}) & =24{x}^{2}-2x\\operatorname{ln}(y) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{y(24{x}^{2}-2x\\operatorname{ln}(y))}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =24y-\\frac{2y\\operatorname{ln}(y)}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233118 :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "09438de3-169c-5930-a51b-e7ea865bc196" :question/math-academy-id "q-108681" :question/problem "Find the slope of the tangent at the point $(\\frac{1}{2},\\,2)$ for the curve ${x}^{2}{y}^{3}=2.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the slope at a point, we use implicit differentiation to calculate the derivative of the curve, assuming that $y=y(x).$\n\nNote that to differentiate the ${x}^{2}{y}^{3}$ term, we will need to use the product rule.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}{y}^{3}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2) \\\\ {x}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({y}^{3})+{y}^{3}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}) & =0 \\\\ 3{x}^{2}{y}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+{y}^{3}\\cdot 2x & =0 \\\\ 3{x}^{2}{y}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2x{y}^{3} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{2x{y}^{3}}{3{x}^{2}{y}^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{2y}{3x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe slope of the tangent at the point $(\\frac{1}{2},\\,2)$ is then given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}_{(1/2,\\,2)} \\\\  & ={(-\\frac{2y}{3x})}_{(1/2,\\,2)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2\\cdot 2}{3\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2})} \\\\  & =-\\frac{8}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233130 :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "2fbae22f-6bd7-5d9b-9385-2d221b17f558" :question/math-academy-id "q-110212" :question/problem "The geometric series $2\\,401+343+⋯+7$  can be expressed using sigma notation as" :question/worked-solution "We must express the given series in the form\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{N}{a}_{1}{r}^{n-1},\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{1}$ denotes the first term of the series, $r$ denotes the common ratio, and $N$ denotes the number of terms in the series. In this case, ${a}_{1}=2\\,401.$\n\nTo find $r,$ we find the ratio of the consecutive terms ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{2}:$\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}}=\\frac{343}{2\\,401}=\\frac{1}{7}\n$$\n\nNow we must determine $N$ (the index of the last term). To do this, we use the fact that\n\n$$\n{a}_{N}={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{N-1}.\n$$\n\nTherefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{N} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{N-1} \\\\ 7 & =2\\,401\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{7})}^{N-1} \\\\ \\frac{7}{2\\,401} & ={(\\frac{1}{7})}^{N-1} \\\\ \\frac{1}{343} & =\\frac{1}{{7}^{N-1}} \\\\ 343 & ={7}^{N-1} \\\\ {7}^{3} & ={7}^{N-1} \\\\ 3 & =N-1 \\\\ N & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpressing the geometric series in sigma notation, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\,401+343+⋯+7 & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{4}2\\,401\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{7})}^{n-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233133 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1049ad2b-d3c5-598f-a813-b594700c836e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{15}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478580} {:answer/id #uuid "2b3ce2a3-15e3-5f28-8cd5-6e81df3d84f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478625} {:answer/id #uuid "84826b57-c122-587c-baf5-141a650be923" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478762} {:answer/id #uuid "85d2c120-3987-58fb-b1a8-1cdfdf73f75a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{15}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478765} {:answer/id #uuid "fbeb8da5-4868-5726-bf1c-39c8f81f6da3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478953}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "84826b57-c122-587c-baf5-141a650be923" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478762} :answer-field/id #uuid "2d842570-384d-5f9a-8934-5fc8f5ec2231" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478974}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "cad2b776-429a-5d87-afc3-be9a913d2b9f" :question/math-academy-id "q-110328" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)=3x+\\sqrt{x}.$ Given that $y=f(x)$ is invertible throughout its domain, determine $({f}^{-1})^{′}(2).$" :question/worked-solution "To determine $({f}^{-1})^{′}(2),$ we use the following formula:\n\n$$\n{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(2)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(2))}\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the value of ${f}^{-1}(2).$ To do this, we solve the equation ${f}^{-1}(2)=x.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{-1}(2) & =x \\\\ f(x) & =2 \\\\ 3x+\\sqrt{x} & =2 \\\\ \\sqrt{x} & =2-3x \\\\ x & =4-12x+9{x}^{2} \\\\ 9{x}^{2}-13x+4 & =0 \\\\ (9x-4)(x-1) & =0 \\\\ x & =\\frac{4}{9},\\,1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nChecking the solutions, we notice that only $x=\\frac{4}{9}$ satisfies the equation $3x+\\sqrt{x}=2.$ Therefore, we discard $x=1$ and conclude that ${f}^{-1}(2)=\\frac{4}{9}.$\n\nNow, we calculate ${f}^{′}(\\frac{4}{9}),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =3x+\\sqrt{x} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =3+\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}} \\\\ {f}^{′}(\\frac{4}{9}) & =3+\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{(\\frac{4}{9})}} \\\\ {f}^{′}(\\frac{4}{9}) & =3+\\frac{3}{4} \\\\ {f}^{′}(\\frac{4}{9}) & =\\frac{15}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(1) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(1))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(\\frac{4}{9})} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{15}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233136 :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f0e208b3-c758-5793-bf13-95c2af152c9b" :question/math-academy-id "q-110540" :question/problem "Find ${f}^{'}(x)$  for $f(x)=\\frac{\\operatorname{arcsin}2x}{9}.$" :question/worked-solution "The formula for the derivative of arcsine is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{arcsin}x)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}.\n$$\n\nUsing the chain rule and the formula above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{'}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{\\operatorname{arcsin}2x}{9}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{arcsin}2x) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-(2x)^{2}}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2x) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-4{x}^{2}}}\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{9\\sqrt{1-4{x}^{2}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233174 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0b47ac44-1559-5265-ba44-bd5a6e355495" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{21}}{21}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513558} {:answer/id #uuid "62de7813-7886-5bed-86cd-1ca23e600b30" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "21{x}^{19}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513799} {:answer/id #uuid "65428549-ccd7-5201-8026-265c549f0352" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{21}}{19}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513804} {:answer/id #uuid "7f5af8b6-1444-5218-bbb1-bf6a887d157c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{19}}{19}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513863} {:answer/id #uuid "8cf69104-089b-5eec-8f81-3bf53d5632ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20{x}^{21}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513895}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0b47ac44-1559-5265-ba44-bd5a6e355495" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{21}}{21}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513558} :answer-field/id #uuid "6982cbcb-0bd9-554d-8104-e02dd398a383" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514278}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6e6c66f6-6133-59f6-a79d-0cbf1615accf" :question/math-academy-id "q-113665" :question/problem "Calculate $\\int {x}^{20}\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "We apply the power rule with $n=20$, and we get\n\n$$\n\\int {x}^{20}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{{x}^{20+1}}{20+1}+C=\\frac{{x}^{21}}{21}+C.\n$$\n\nLet's check by differentiating:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{{x}^{21}}{21}+C) &= \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{{x}^{21}}{21})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\ &= \\frac{1}{21}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{21})+0 \\\\ &= \\frac{1}{21}\\cdot 21{x}^{20} \\\\ &= {x}^{20}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233175 :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7949e7bf-09d1-5ce9-ac3c-0d7f26b2e6c4" :question/math-academy-id "q-113670" :question/problem "Calculate $F(x)$ given that $f(x)={x}^{17}$ and ${F}^{'}(x)=f(x).$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that integration is the opposite of differentiation. So, if ${F}^{'}(x)=f(x),$ then integrating both sides of this equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{'}(x) & =f(x) \\\\ \\int {F}^{'}(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ F(x) & =\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe apply the power rule with $n=17$, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(x) & =\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int {x}^{17}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{17+1}}{17+1}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{18}}{18}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's check by differentiating:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{{x}^{18}}{18}+C) &= \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{{x}^{18}}{18})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\ &= \\frac{1}{18}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{18})+0 \\\\ &= \\frac{1}{18}\\cdot 18\\,{x}^{17} \\\\ &= {x}^{17}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233176 :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "353f4779-4251-5a93-b99f-1eda6757b24d" :question/math-academy-id "q-113674" :question/problem "Find the antiderivative of ${x}^{1/4}.$" :question/worked-solution "We can apply the power rule with $n=\\frac{1}{4}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {x}^{1/4}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{{x}^{1/4+1}}{(\\frac{1}{4}+1)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{5/4}}{(\\frac{5}{4})}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{4{x}^{5/4}}{5}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAgain, let's check by differentiating:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{4{x}^{5/4}}{5}+C) &= \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{4{x}^{5/4}}{5})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\ &= \\frac{4}{5}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{5/4})+0 \\\\ &= \\frac{4}{5}\\cdot \\frac{5}{4}{x}^{5/4-1} \\\\ &= {x}^{1/4}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233177 :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "1eab1e18-d0df-58e6-917b-50a1d74ed3f6" :question/math-academy-id "q-113680" :question/problem "Calculate the integral of $\\sqrt[5]{{x}^{3}}.$" :question/worked-solution "We start by writing the root as a rational power, $\\sqrt[5]{{x}^{3}}={x}^{3/5}.$ So, we can apply the power rule with $n=\\frac{3}{5}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\sqrt[5]{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {x}^{3/5}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{3/5+1}}{(\\frac{3}{5}+1)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{8/5}}{(\\frac{8}{5})}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{5{x}^{8/5}}{8}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAgain, let's check by differentiating:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{5{x}^{8/5}}{8}+C) &= \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{5{x}^{8/5}}{8})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\ &= \\frac{5}{8}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{8/5})+0 \\\\ &= \\frac{5}{8}\\cdot \\frac{8}{5}{x}^{8/5-1} \\\\ &= {x}^{3/5}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233178 :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "63703f37-ab84-53aa-922b-b2e419a9f1a5" :question/math-academy-id "q-113695" :question/problem "Find the antiderivative of $\\sqrt[5]{{x}^{2}}.$" :question/worked-solution "We start by writing the root as a rational power, $\\sqrt[5]{{x}^{2}}={x}^{2/5}.$ So, we can apply the power rule with $n=\\frac{2}{5}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\sqrt[5]{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {x}^{2/5}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2/5+1}}{(\\frac{2}{5}+1)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{7/5}}{(\\frac{7}{5})}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{5{x}^{7/5}}{7}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAgain, let's check by differentiating:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{5{x}^{7/5}}{7}+C) &= \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{5{x}^{7/5}}{7})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\ &= \\frac{5}{7}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{7/5})+0 \\\\ &= \\frac{5}{7}\\cdot \\frac{7}{5}{x}^{7/5-1} \\\\ &= {x}^{2/5}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233179 :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "25edeff2-faa4-50c4-bbfd-3291d7ac7694" :question/math-academy-id "q-113705" :question/problem "Given that $f(x)=\\frac{1}{{x}^{4/3}}$ and that ${F}^{'}(x)=f(x)$, calculate $F(x).$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that integration is the opposite of differentiation. So, if ${F}^{'}(x)=f(x),$ then integrating both sides of this equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{'}(x) & =f(x) \\\\ \\int {F}^{'}(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ F(x) & =\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{1}{{x}^{4/3}}={x}^{-4/3}.$ So, we can use the power rule with $n=-\\frac{4}{3}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(x) & =\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int {x}^{-4/3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{-4/3+1}}{(-\\frac{4}{3}+1)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{-1/3}}{(-\\frac{1}{3})}+C \\\\  & =-3{x}^{-1/3}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{{x}^{1/3}}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233180 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6f05efc8-fec2-570b-87ce-c010c59354fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{15{x}^{5}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534496} {:answer/id #uuid "867ff7cf-a3a9-5f4e-9f81-a86ed899e17f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{3{x}^{5}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534556} {:answer/id #uuid "86db9c05-054a-512f-9076-5d6601562ba3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{21{x}^{7}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534557} {:answer/id #uuid "aa104223-6a26-5471-ac67-5cfc91cda9db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{7}{3{x}^{7}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534646} {:answer/id #uuid "ed5eb29d-e2f7-5502-9cac-86d15c20177a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{5{x}^{5}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534792}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6f05efc8-fec2-570b-87ce-c010c59354fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{15{x}^{5}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534496} :answer-field/id #uuid "491fcc02-859b-58ab-a165-365def78f7fd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534870}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fe44f85b-e8b3-5b91-9050-93c115ce76be" :question/math-academy-id "q-113733" :question/problem "Calculate $\\int \\frac{1}{3{x}^{6}}\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "We can just take the constant factor out of the integral, and then apply the power rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{3{x}^{6}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{3}\\int {x}^{-6}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot (\\frac{{x}^{-6+1}}{-6+1})+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot (\\frac{{x}^{-5}}{-5})+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{15}\\cdot {x}^{-5}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{15{x}^{5}}+C\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233181 :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4687a896-1912-54fd-bab7-72fd9b8cd442" :question/math-academy-id "q-113734" :question/problem "Calculate $\\int \\frac{3}{2{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y.$" :question/worked-solution "We can just take the constant factor out of the integral, and then apply the power rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{3}{2{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y & =\\frac{3}{2}\\int {y}^{-2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot (\\frac{{y}^{(-2+1)}}{-2+1})+C \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot (\\frac{{y}^{-1}}{-1})+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot {y}^{-1}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2y}+C\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233182 :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6908d93f-16b8-5efc-a68c-9ce4857c5395" :question/math-academy-id "q-113737" :question/problem "Calculate $\\int \\frac{5}{{x}^{11}}\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "We can just take the constant factor out of the integral, and then apply the power rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{5}{{x}^{11}}\\,\\text{d}x & =5\\int {x}^{-11}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =5\\cdot (\\frac{{x}^{-11+1}}{-11+1})+C \\\\  & =5\\cdot (\\frac{{x}^{-10}}{-10})+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{10}\\cdot {x}^{-10}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2{x}^{10}}+C\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233186 :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "8d0c3f56-ed74-5e4d-bb18-eac56541b5e8" :question/math-academy-id "q-113781" :question/problem "Find the antiderivative of $\\sqrt[4]{x}-{x}^{5}.$" :question/worked-solution "Applying the sum rule, we compute the sum of the integrals of each function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\sqrt[4]{x}-{x}^{5}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {x}^{1/4}\\,\\text{d}x-\\int {x}^{5}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{1/4+1}}{(\\frac{1}{4}+1)}-\\frac{{x}^{5+1}}{(5+1)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{5/4}}{(\\frac{5}{4})}-\\frac{{x}^{6}}{(6)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\sqrt[4]{{x}^{5}}}{5}-\\frac{{x}^{6}}{6}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233196 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9848c8b6-63aa-585c-9c3f-f09cf6a49bc9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}[{e}^{k/n}\\cdot \\frac{4}{n}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386725} {:answer/id #uuid "4af5c4a2-1d06-5974-840c-84a0c9369afb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}[{e}^{4k/n}\\cdot \\frac{1}{n}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386726} {:answer/id #uuid "0436ef1f-f984-5be7-bbed-df86ab8e327f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}[{e}^{2k/n}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2n}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386727} {:answer/id #uuid "e02ec00a-498c-51f8-80b1-0c65fcdd05c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}[{e}^{2k/n}\\cdot \\frac{2}{n}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386728} {:answer/id #uuid "092157f2-38d5-50ad-be92-7e9d8c56698d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}[{e}^{k/n}\\cdot \\frac{2}{n}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386729}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e02ec00a-498c-51f8-80b1-0c65fcdd05c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}[{e}^{2k/n}\\cdot \\frac{2}{n}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386728} :answer-field/id #uuid "a11b3b07-1feb-5e0e-9468-621e3b27f6c7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386724}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4385816f-7b9c-5f55-bc1f-96c9f8bc0f20" :question/math-academy-id "q-113927" :question/problem "What is ${\\int }_{0}^{2}{e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x$ expressed as the limit of a right Riemann sum?" :question/worked-solution "The definite integral can be defined in terms of the limit of a right Riemann sum as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}f({x}_{k})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{k}=a+kΔx$ and $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}.$\n\nIn the definite integral\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{2}{e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nthe function is $f(x)={e}^{x},$ the lower bound is $a=0$ and the upper bound is $b=2.$ So, we have\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{2-0}{n}=\\frac{2}{n}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}f({x}_{k})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}[f(0+k\\cdot \\frac{2}{n})\\cdot \\frac{2}{n}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}[f(\\frac{2k}{n})\\cdot \\frac{2}{n}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}[{e}^{2k/n}\\cdot \\frac{2}{n}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233220 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "13f6e06d-ad65-5cde-96a5-9069e12b1b26" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+2)^{2}=3(y+4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247603} {:answer/id #uuid "1863addd-8a0a-59e0-892b-a7d6a7103de6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-2)^{2}=-\\frac{4}{3}(y-4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247641} {:answer/id #uuid "276176b3-7f2b-5c88-8efa-a992136a89ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-2)^{2}=-\\frac{1}{3}(y-4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247773} {:answer/id #uuid "4ad42424-46f6-5c6c-9935-0f7c0b4b4f0f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+2)^{2}=-\\frac{1}{3}(y+4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248093} {:answer/id #uuid "4e29f8bb-49b9-5aed-8f9b-d0e87040d562" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-4)^{2}=4(y-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248125}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1863addd-8a0a-59e0-892b-a7d6a7103de6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-2)^{2}=-\\frac{4}{3}(y-4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247641} :answer-field/id #uuid "4f2f573c-7892-568b-af35-7446ef231fcc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249817}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4d4efadb-43b5-5c03-b6a8-35763b2fb759" :question/math-academy-id "q-115511" :question/problem "Find the equation of the downward-opening parabola that has its vertex at $(2,4)$ and which passes through the point $(4,1).$" :question/worked-solution "For any upward or downward-opening parabola with its vertex at $(h,k),$ the general standard equation is given by\n\n$$\n(x-h)^{2}=4p(y-k).\n$$\n\nHere, the vertex has coordinates $(h,k)=(2,4).$ Substituting these into the general standard equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-2)^{2} & =4p(y-4).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are left to find the value of $p.$ We know that the given parabola also passes through the point $(4,1),$ so we can substitute $x=4$ and $y=1$ into the standard equation and solve for $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-2)^{2} & =4p(y-4) \\\\ (4-2)^{2} & =4p(1-4) \\\\ 4 & =-12p \\\\ p & =-\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the parabola is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-2)^{2} & =4p(y-4) \\\\ (x-2)^{2} & =4(-\\frac{1}{3})(y-4) \\\\ (x-2)^{2} & =-\\frac{4}{3}(y-4).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233221 :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "edff791a-4805-5dd3-8658-8c4b650028a4" :question/math-academy-id "q-115521" :question/problem "Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{{ln}^{2}x}{x}.$" :question/worked-solution "Here the limits of the numerator and denominator are ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{ln}^{2}x=\\infty $ and ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }x=\\infty ,$ respectively, so we have the case $\\frac{\\infty }{\\infty }.$\n\nApplying L'Hopital's rule, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{{ln}^{2}x}{x} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{[{ln}^{2}x]^{'}}{{[x]}^{'}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}x}{x})}{1} \\\\  & =-{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}x}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis time, the resulting limit still gives the indeterminate form $\\frac{\\infty }{\\infty }.$ To find the limit, we apply L'Hopital's rule once again. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\operatorname{ln}x)^{2}}{x} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}x}{x} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{[2\\operatorname{ln}x]^{'}}{[x]^{'}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{2}{x})}{1} \\\\  & =2{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{x} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233222 :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8fb3f6e8-09cd-5887-a45a-f9645361e3b2" :question/math-academy-id "q-115525" :question/problem "Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to {\\frac{\\pi }{2}}^{-}}\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}(\\operatorname{cos}x)}{\\operatorname{tan}x}.$" :question/worked-solution "Here the limits of the numerator and denominator are ${lim}_{x\\to {\\frac{\\pi }{2}}^{-}}\\operatorname{ln}(\\operatorname{cos}x)=-\\infty $ and ${lim}_{x\\to {\\frac{\\pi }{2}}^{-}}\\operatorname{tan}x=\\infty ,$ respectively, so we have the case $\\frac{-\\infty }{\\infty }.$\n\nApplying L'Hopital's rule, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {\\frac{\\pi }{2}}^{-}}\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}(\\operatorname{cos}x)}{\\operatorname{tan}x} & ={lim}_{x\\to {\\frac{\\pi }{2}}^{-}}\\frac{[\\operatorname{ln}(\\operatorname{cos}x)]^{'}}{{[\\operatorname{tan}x]}^{'}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to {\\frac{\\pi }{2}}^{-}}\\frac{(-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}x}{\\operatorname{cos}x})}{{sec}^{2}x} \\\\  & =-{lim}_{x\\to {\\frac{\\pi }{2}}^{-}}\\frac{(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}x}{\\operatorname{cos}x})}{(\\frac{1}{{cos}^{2}x})} \\\\  & =-{lim}_{x\\to {\\frac{\\pi }{2}}^{-}}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}x{cos}^{2}x}{\\operatorname{cos}x} \\\\  & =-{lim}_{x\\to {\\frac{\\pi }{2}}^{-}}\\operatorname{sin}x\\operatorname{cos}x \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233223 :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "3c0e609b-967f-5ab0-a92a-e591ea1aedc7" :question/math-academy-id "q-115529" :question/problem "Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{\\operatorname{cot}2x}{\\operatorname{cot}x}.$" :question/worked-solution "Here the limits of the numerator and denominator are ${lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\operatorname{cot}2x=\\infty $ and ${lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\operatorname{cot}x=\\infty ,$ respectively, so we have the case $\\frac{\\infty }{\\infty }.$\n\nApplying L'Hopital's rule, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{\\operatorname{cot}2x}{\\operatorname{cot}x} & ={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{[\\operatorname{cot}2x]^{'}}{[\\operatorname{cot}x]^{'}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{(-2{csc}^{2}2x)}{-{csc}^{2}x} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{2\\cdot (\\frac{1}{{sin}^{2}2x})}{(\\frac{1}{{sin}^{2}x})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{2{sin}^{2}x}{{sin}^{2}2x} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{2{sin}^{2}x}{{(2\\operatorname{sin}x\\operatorname{cos}x)}^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{2{sin}^{2}x}{4{sin}^{2}x{cos}^{2}x} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{2\\cancel{{sin}^{2}x}}{4\\cancel{{sin}^{2}x}{cos}^{2}x} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{1}{2{cos}^{2}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2{cos}^{2}0} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233226 :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "75157a6e-bf73-5daf-9fa9-84f0f942f081" :question/math-academy-id "q-11602" :question/problem "If $y=2{x}^{3}\\sqrt{2{x}^{2}+7},$ find the value of $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ at $x=2.$" :question/worked-solution "This question requires the product rule. Let $u(x)=2{x}^{3}$ and $v(x)=\\sqrt{2{x}^{2}+7}.$ Then\n\n$$\n{u}^{'}(x)=6{x}^{2},\\,{v}^{'}(x)=\\frac{2x}{\\sqrt{2{x}^{2}+7}}.\n$$\n\nNote that we used the chain rule to differentiate $v.$\n\nNow, applying the product rule and simplifying, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={u}^{'}(x)\\cdot v(x)+u(x)\\cdot {v}^{'}(x) \\\\  & =(6{x}^{2})(\\sqrt{2{x}^{2}+7})+(2{x}^{3})(\\frac{2x}{\\sqrt{2{x}^{2}+7}}) \\\\  & =6{x}^{2}\\sqrt{2{x}^{2}+7}+\\frac{4{x}^{4}}{\\sqrt{2{x}^{2}+7}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the value of $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ at $x=2$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})|}_{x=2} & =6(2)^{2}\\sqrt{2(2)^{2}+7}+\\frac{4(2)^{4}}{\\sqrt{2(2)^{2}+7}} \\\\  & =24\\sqrt{15}+\\frac{64}{\\sqrt{15}} \\\\  & =\\frac{360+64}{\\sqrt{15}} \\\\  & =\\frac{424}{\\sqrt{15}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233227 :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e995c495-b172-523a-b382-0b2801a175c6" :question/math-academy-id "q-11603" :question/problem "If $y=3{x}^{2}{({x}^{3}-1)}^{4}$, find the value of $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ at $x=1.$" :question/worked-solution "This question requires the product rule. Let $u(x)=3{x}^{2}$ and $v(x)={({x}^{3}-1)}^{4}.$ Then\n\n$$\n{u}^{'}(x)=6x,\\,{v}^{'}(x)=12{x}^{2}{({x}^{3}-1)}^{3}.\n$$\n\nNote that we used the chain rule to differentiate $v.$\n\nNow, applying the product rule and simplifying, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={u}^{'}(x)\\cdot v(x)+u(x)\\cdot {v}^{'}(x) \\\\  & =(6x)(({x}^{3}-1)^{4})+(3{x}^{2})(12{x}^{2}({x}^{3}-1)^{3}) \\\\  & =6x({x}^{3}-1)^{4}+36{x}^{4}{({x}^{3}-1)}^{3} \\\\  & =(6x({x}^{3}-1)+36{x}^{4})\\,{({x}^{3}-1)}^{3} \\\\  & =(6{x}^{4}-6x+36{x}^{4})\\,{({x}^{3}-1)}^{3} \\\\  & =(42{x}^{4}-6x)\\,{({x}^{3}-1)}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo evaluate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ at $x=1$, we substitute $x=1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})|}_{x=1} & =(42\\cdot {1}^{4}-6\\cdot 1){({1}^{3}-1)}^{3} \\\\  & =36\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233230 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "36d0b2e7-6b1e-5c39-947f-21883b5f48ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}[{(\\frac{4j}{n}+1)}^{3}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552975} {:answer/id #uuid "994192dd-582e-50fe-98a3-038c82cfc67d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\sum }_{j=0}^{n}[{(\\frac{4}{n})}^{4}{(j+1)}^{3}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553178} {:answer/id #uuid "9c82cd81-4ecb-542d-ae56-556d01765358" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}[{(\\frac{4}{n})}^{4}{j}^{3}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553186} {:answer/id #uuid "a7034dd5-0b93-59ba-85f4-8a88fce11c84" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}[\\frac{4}{n}{(\\frac{4j}{n}+1)}^{3}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553200} {:answer/id #uuid "ae50862f-a25d-563a-a92d-0d0178b72923" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n-1}[\\frac{4}{n}{(\\frac{4j}{n}+1)}^{3}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553219}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a7034dd5-0b93-59ba-85f4-8a88fce11c84" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}[\\frac{4}{n}{(\\frac{4j}{n}+1)}^{3}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553200} :answer-field/id #uuid "91f26888-434b-5c5c-a749-e627db784a28" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553444}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "d0dd25ca-44a0-5275-b710-e7c2a70a0387" :question/math-academy-id "q-116627" :question/problem "The area under the curve $y=(x+1)^{3}$ between $x=0$ and $x=4$ and the $x$-axis is approximated by a right Riemann sum using $n$ subintervals of equal length. What is the expression for the Riemann sum in sigma notation?" :question/worked-solution "The right Riemann sum for $y=f(x)$ with a regular step size in sigma notation is\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}f({x}_{j})Δx,\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{j}=a+jΔx$ with $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}.$ \n\nWe're told that there are $n$ subintervals. The endpoints indicate that $a=0$ and $b=4.$ So, we have\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{4-0}{n}=\\frac{4}{n}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using sigma notation, the right Riemann sum can be expressed as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}f({x}_{j})Δx & ={\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}f(a+jΔx)Δx \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}[f(0+j\\cdot \\frac{4}{n})\\cdot \\frac{4}{n}] \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}[f(\\frac{4j}{n})\\cdot \\frac{4}{n}] \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}[\\frac{4}{n}{(\\frac{4j}{n}+1)}^{3}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233244 :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b56a14ee-3761-5cbe-b520-0f78b7eb7630" :question/math-academy-id "q-117401" :question/problem "$\\operatorname{arcsin}(\\frac{1}{2})=$" :question/worked-solution "First, note that the range of $\\theta =\\operatorname{arcsin}x$ is given by\n\n$$\n-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nNote that the angle $\\theta $ spans the first and fourth quadrants.\n\nSince $\\frac{1}{2}$ is positive, our answer is an angle that lies in the first quadrant, where sine is positive.\n\nNow, let's take a look at the unit circle.\n\n![](images/a3/a33ea7c6d1aa7cd7e54376aefb2af4347da59a61ecaabdbf4964d41b9661870a.png)\n\nFrom the values in the first quadrant, we see that\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{\\pi }{6})=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{arcsin}(\\frac{1}{2})=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233248 :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7b1e99d2-4571-5eb0-b1cd-3cfd5a30a159" :question/math-academy-id "q-117583" :question/problem "What is the period of the function $f(x)=\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{8x}{3}+{20}^{∘})?$" :question/worked-solution "Given a function in the form $y=A\\operatorname{sin}(Bx+C)+D,$ the period is $\\frac{{360}^{∘}}{B}.$\n\nIn our case, we have that $B=\\frac{8}{3}.$ Therefore, the period is $\\frac{{360}^{∘}}{(\\frac{8}{3})}={135}^{∘}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233260 :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "cc93e0f3-9a67-5610-9532-f7ede130b948" :question/math-academy-id "q-119100" :question/problem "Simplify the expression $\\operatorname{sec}\\theta +\\frac{\\operatorname{tan}\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta }.$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's interpret the fraction as a division:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\operatorname{tan}\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta }=\\operatorname{tan}\\theta ÷\\operatorname{sin}\\theta \n$$\n\nThen, we recall the identity $\\operatorname{tan}\\theta =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta },$ and we substitute it into the above expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tan}\\theta ÷\\operatorname{sin}\\theta  & =(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta })÷\\operatorname{sin}\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we turn the division into multiplication, and simplify the expression as much as possible:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sec}\\theta +(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta })÷\\operatorname{sin}\\theta  & =\\operatorname{sec}\\theta +\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta }\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta } \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sec}\\theta +\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta \\cdot 1}{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{sin}\\theta } \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sec}\\theta +\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{sin}\\theta } \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sec}\\theta +\\frac{\\cancel{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta }}{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta \\cdot \\cancel{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta }} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sec}\\theta +\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta } \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sec}\\theta +\\operatorname{sec}\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sec}\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\operatorname{sec}\\theta +\\frac{\\operatorname{tan}\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta }=2\\operatorname{sec}\\theta .$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233262 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/feedback "The negative coefficient remains negative: d[-3x^2]/dx = -6x." :answer/id #uuid "3b2b9b31-ff69-5fd5-b05e-2f7659ff135f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{x}+6x+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186408180} {:answer/feedback "Differentiate term by term: d[5 ln(x)]/dx = 5/x, d[-3x^2]/dx = -6x, and d[2x]/dx = 2." :answer/id #uuid "0d5c95ed-e8ad-537b-a8d6-7a28ab66e027" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{x}-6x+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186408181} {:answer/feedback "The coefficient 5 multiplies the derivative of ln(x): d[5 ln(x)]/dx = 5/x, not 1/x." :answer/id #uuid "91dae272-a571-532b-b8c9-5f1a0d446765" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{x}-6x+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186408182} {:answer/feedback "Differentiate the quadratic term as well: d[-3x^2]/dx = -6x; the original -3x^2 term cannot remain in the derivative." :answer/id #uuid "3b96cf13-3041-512c-817b-bce2b6cd16ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{x}-3x^{2}+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186408183} {:answer/feedback "The power rule gives d[-3x^2]/dx = -3(2x) = -6x. Include the factor 2 from the exponent." :answer/id #uuid "441d1cc7-ac98-5400-9e91-3512f2bc8afd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{x}-3x+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186408184}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/feedback "Differentiate term by term: d[5 ln(x)]/dx = 5/x, d[-3x^2]/dx = -6x, and d[2x]/dx = 2." :answer/id #uuid "0d5c95ed-e8ad-537b-a8d6-7a28ab66e027" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{x}-6x+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186408181} :answer-field/id #uuid "11eacdbf-0599-50c9-89ae-2687d890483f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186408185}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0a394889-e060-5e16-b9db-b5d3312e9a55" :question/math-academy-id "q-11914" :question/problem "Given that $y=5\\operatorname{ln}x-3{x}^{2}+2x$, find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.$" :question/worked-solution "Taking the derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(5\\operatorname{ln}x-3{x}^{2}+2x) \\\\  & =5(\\frac{1}{x})-3\\cdot 2x+2 \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{x}-6x+2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233264 :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "65d93c7b-d882-5d2a-b8f9-e6a3f66334e8" :question/math-academy-id "q-119266" :question/problem "Simplify the trigonometric expression $\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta -\\operatorname{sec}\\theta \\operatorname{cot}\\theta \\operatorname{sin}\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta -1}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's interpret the fraction as a division:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta -\\operatorname{sec}\\theta \\operatorname{cot}\\theta \\operatorname{sin}\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta -1}=(\\operatorname{cos}\\theta -\\operatorname{sec}\\theta \\operatorname{cot}\\theta \\operatorname{sin}\\theta )÷(\\operatorname{cos}\\theta -1)\n$$\n\nThen, we recall the identities $\\operatorname{cot}\\theta =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta }$ and $\\operatorname{sec}\\theta =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta },$ and we substitute them into the above expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\operatorname{cos}\\theta -\\operatorname{sec}\\theta \\operatorname{cot}\\theta \\operatorname{sin}\\theta )÷(\\operatorname{cos}\\theta -1) & =(\\operatorname{cos}\\theta -\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta }\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta }\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}\\theta )÷(\\operatorname{cos}\\theta -1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we simplify, turn the division into multiplication, and then simplify again:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\operatorname{cos}\\theta -\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta }\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta }\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}\\theta )÷(\\operatorname{cos}\\theta -1) & =(\\operatorname{cos}\\theta -\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{sin}\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{sin}\\theta })÷(\\operatorname{cos}\\theta -1) \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{cos}\\theta -\\frac{\\cancel{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta }\\cdot \\cancel{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta }}{\\cancel{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta }\\cdot \\cancel{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta }})÷(\\operatorname{cos}\\theta -1) \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{cos}\\theta -1)÷(\\operatorname{cos}\\theta -1) \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{cos}\\theta -1)\\cdot (\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta -1}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta -1}{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta -1} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta -1}}{\\cancel{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta -1}} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta -\\operatorname{sec}\\theta \\operatorname{cot}\\theta \\operatorname{sin}\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta -1}=1.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233267 :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "429c60b9-5038-573f-9e54-efbf678a8db5" :question/math-academy-id "q-11939" :question/problem "Given that $y=x\\operatorname{ln}3x,$ find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.$" :question/worked-solution "This question requires the product rule. Let $u(x)=x$ and $v(x)=\\operatorname{ln}3x.$ Then\n\n$$\n{u}^{'}(x)=1,\\,{v}^{'}(x)=\\frac{1}{x}.\n$$\n\nNote that we used the chain rule to differentiate $v.$\n\nNow, applying the product rule and simplifying, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={u}^{'}(x)\\cdot v(x)+u(x)\\cdot {v}^{'}(x) \\\\  & =(1)(\\operatorname{ln}3x)+(x)(\\frac{1}{x}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}3x+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233270 :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "42e12840-e254-5d23-8205-fd6d9545a4e4" :question/math-academy-id "q-119481" :question/problem "Find the exact value of $\\operatorname{cos}(-{75}^{∘}).$\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $-{75}^{∘}={60}^{∘}-{135}^{∘}.$*" :question/worked-solution "Recall the difference formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}(x-y)=\\operatorname{cos}x\\operatorname{cos}y+\\operatorname{sin}x\\operatorname{sin}y\n$$\n\nApplying the difference formula for cosine with $x={60}^{∘}$ and $y={135}^{∘},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}(-{75}^{∘}) & =\\operatorname{cos}({60}^{∘}-{135}^{∘}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}{60}^{∘}\\operatorname{cos}{135}^{∘}+\\operatorname{sin}{60}^{∘}\\operatorname{sin}{135}^{∘} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})+\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}+\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{6}-\\sqrt{2}}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233276 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4bf6d318-67a3-589f-aa06-712a4c979552" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2(m+n)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512262} {:answer/id #uuid "5466b133-5ea5-525f-8cd7-1a549d321d24" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{2}}(m+n)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512288} {:answer/id #uuid "8a9a8734-2fa1-5cd5-97d6-c6ffcae6ba4c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}(m+n)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512426} {:answer/id #uuid "9b35183a-e748-54ed-8dd3-0bcbe740b891" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{17}}(m+n)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512466} {:answer/id #uuid "ed844729-fa98-5650-835d-6732a8a4b167" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}}(m+n)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512675}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9b35183a-e748-54ed-8dd3-0bcbe740b891" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{17}}(m+n)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512466} :answer-field/id #uuid "5749cf60-a3f1-5876-9c2b-03d5d66464f0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512772}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "45f48cf6-e519-5407-abc6-4bbd59c8789a" :question/math-academy-id "q-119805" :question/problem "![](images/95/95c30eab094a24beb6976262167401efa973dbac12781c69d074eb14e12e2743.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. Which of the following is a unit vector that is parallel to $m+n?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we have to find $m+n.$ According to the triangle law of addition, the resultant $m+n$ goes from the tail of $m$ to the head of $n:$ \n\n![](images/87/87647211f77776c5f5f740c205fafb150eef1b9d3ebefb82df73a9fdcd953bdb.png)\n\nIn $△AHB$, we have $∠AHB={90}^{∘}$, $AH=0.5=\\frac{1}{2}$ and $BH=2.$ So, the Pythagorean theorem gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|m+n| & =AB \\\\  & =\\sqrt{A{H}^{2}+B{H}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+{2}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{4}+4} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{17}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the magnitude of $m+n$ is $\\frac{\\sqrt{17}}{2}.$ Consequently, the unit vector is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u & =\\frac{1}{|m+n|}\\cdot (m+n) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{17}}(m+n).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233278 :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "807c1c95-6fea-5d65-a59b-b497c894ecc9" :question/math-academy-id "q-119947" :question/problem "Simplify ${(\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{\\pi }{6})+\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{\\pi }{6}))}^{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we expand the parenthesis and use the Pythagorean identity:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{\\pi }{6})+\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{\\pi }{6}))}^{2} & ={sin}^{2}(\\frac{\\pi }{6})+2\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{\\pi }{6})\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{\\pi }{6})+{cos}^{2}(\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =[{sin}^{2}(\\frac{\\pi }{6})+{cos}^{2}(\\frac{\\pi }{6})]+2\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{\\pi }{6})\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =1+2\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{\\pi }{6})\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{\\pi }{6})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's recall the double-angle formula for sine: \n\n$$\n2\\operatorname{sin}x\\operatorname{cos}x=\\operatorname{sin}2x\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ into the above, we get\n\n$$\n2\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{\\pi }{6})\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{\\pi }{6})=\\operatorname{sin}(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{6})=\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{\\pi }{3}).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{(\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{\\pi }{6})+\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{\\pi }{6}))}^{2}=1+2\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{\\pi }{6})\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{\\pi }{6})=1+\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{\\pi }{3}).\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233280 :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b07b8b2a-0233-5748-8982-4707b89b5f6a" :question/math-academy-id "q-120033" :question/problem "Find the exact value of $\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{5\\pi }{8})\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{5\\pi }{8}).$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the double-angle formula for sine: \n\n$$\n2\\operatorname{sin}x\\operatorname{cos}x=\\operatorname{sin}2x\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=\\frac{5\\pi }{8}$ into the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{5\\pi }{8})\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{5\\pi }{8}) & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{5\\pi }{8})\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{5\\pi }{8}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}(2\\cdot \\frac{5\\pi }{8}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{5\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{5\\pi }{8})\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{5\\pi }{8})=-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233281 :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "22b1843d-4007-53d9-ab88-b98d3616888d" :question/math-academy-id "q-120036" :question/problem "Find the exact value of ${(\\operatorname{sin}({165}^{∘})-\\operatorname{cos}({165}^{∘}))}^{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we expand the parenthesis and use the Pythagorean identity.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\operatorname{sin}({165}^{∘})-\\operatorname{cos}({165}^{∘}))}^{2} & ={sin}^{2}({165}^{∘})-2\\operatorname{sin}({165}^{∘})\\operatorname{cos}({165}^{∘})+{cos}^{2}({165}^{∘}) \\\\  & =[{sin}^{2}({165}^{∘})+{cos}^{2}({165}^{∘})]-2\\operatorname{sin}({165}^{∘})\\operatorname{cos}({165}^{∘}) \\\\  & =1-2\\operatorname{sin}({165}^{∘})\\operatorname{cos}({165}^{∘})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's recall the double-angle formula for sine: \n\n$$\n2\\operatorname{sin}x\\operatorname{cos}x=\\operatorname{sin}2x\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x={165}^{∘}$ into the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{sin}({165}^{∘})\\operatorname{cos}({165}^{∘}) & =\\operatorname{sin}(2\\cdot {165}^{∘}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}({330}^{∘}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we obtain\n\n$$\n1-2\\operatorname{sin}({165}^{∘})\\operatorname{cos}({165}^{∘})=1-(-\\frac{1}{2})=\\frac{3}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{(\\operatorname{sin}({165}^{∘})+\\operatorname{cos}({165}^{∘}))}^{2}=\\frac{3}{2}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233283 :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "19bb82ee-2583-57ae-92ec-48971505d385" :question/math-academy-id "q-120094" :question/problem "Find the exact value of $\\frac{\\operatorname{sec}(\\frac{\\pi }{8})}{\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{\\pi }{16})\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{\\pi }{16})}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the double-angle formula for sine: \n\n$$\n2\\operatorname{sin}x\\operatorname{cos}x=\\operatorname{sin}2x\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=\\frac{\\pi }{16}$ into the above, we get\n\n$$\n2\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{\\pi }{16})\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{\\pi }{16})=\\operatorname{sin}(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{16})\n$$\n\nwhich simplifies as\n\n$$\n2\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{\\pi }{16})\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{\\pi }{16})=\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{\\pi }{8}).\n$$\n\nNow, we can rewrite the given expression using the above, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\operatorname{sec}(\\frac{\\pi }{8})}{\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{\\pi }{16})\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{\\pi }{16})} & =\\frac{2\\operatorname{sec}(\\frac{\\pi }{8})}{2\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{\\pi }{16})\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{\\pi }{16})} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\operatorname{sec}(\\frac{\\pi }{8})}{\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{\\pi }{8})} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{\\pi }{8})\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{\\pi }{8})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, using the double-angle formula once more with $x=\\frac{\\pi }{8}$, we can further simplify the above expression: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{\\pi }{8})\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{\\pi }{8})} & =\\frac{4}{2\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{\\pi }{8})\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{\\pi }{8})} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{\\operatorname{sin}(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{8})} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233294 :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "5abbd63d-f879-5d3b-b710-d5644437547b" :question/math-academy-id "q-121912" :question/problem "Consider the equation $\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{tan}(\\frac{3x}{2})+1=0$ for ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘}.$ If the exact solutions ${x}_{1},{x}_{2}$ and ${x}_{3}$ are written in ascending order, what is the numerical value of ${x}_{2}+{x}_{3}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{tan}(\\frac{3x}{2})+1 & =0 \\\\ \\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{tan}(\\frac{3x}{2}) & =-1 \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}(\\frac{3x}{2}) & =-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}(\\frac{3x}{2}) & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe will find the general solution to the equation, and then generate all of the solutions in the required domain.\n\nThe general solution to our equation is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3x}{2} & =-{30}^{∘}+n\\cdot {180}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving our equation for $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x & =-{60}^{∘}+n\\cdot {360}^{∘} \\\\ x & =-\\frac{{60}^{∘}}{3}+\\frac{n\\cdot {360}^{∘}}{3} \\\\ x & =-{20}^{∘}+n\\cdot {120}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe set $n=0,\\pm 1,\\pm 2,…,$ until we have generated all solutions in the domain ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘}.$\n\n- Setting $n=1$ gives $x={100}^{∘}$\n\n- Setting $n=2$ gives $x={220}^{∘}$\n\n- Setting $n=3$ gives $x={340}^{∘}$\n\nAll other values of $n$ generate solutions outside the domain ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘}.$ So, the solutions ${x}_{1},{x}_{2}$ and ${x}_{3}$ (written in ascending order) are\n\n$$\nx={100}^{∘},{220}^{∘},{340}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the numerical value of ${x}_{2}+{x}_{3}$ is $220+340=560.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233305 :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4b3ecaa5-c4d7-519b-86fc-d3ccb5b1aca7" :question/math-academy-id "q-123583" :question/problem "Find the value of $\\operatorname{tan}\\pi +\\operatorname{cot}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$" :question/worked-solution "The angles $\\pi $ and $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ are quadrantal angles. Let's draw out the quadrantal angles on the unit circle.\n\n![](images/79/792a778286533d589a3410223aaf197b07e64ea77566042d7bb8ac13fbad9768.png)\n\nLet's first consider the angle $\\pi .$ From the unit circle, we see that the angle $\\pi $ corresponds to the point $(-1,0).$\n\n- Since the cosine of the angle corresponds to the $x$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{cos}\\pi =-1.$\n\n- Since the sine of the angle corresponds to the $y$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{sin}\\pi =0.$\n\nTherefore, using the fact that $\\operatorname{tan}\\theta =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}\\pi =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}\\pi }{\\operatorname{cos}\\pi }=\\frac{0}{-1}=0.\n$$\n\nNow, let's consider the angle $\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ From the unit circle, we see that the angle $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ corresponds to the point $(0,1).$\n\n- Since the cosine of the angle corresponds to the $x$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=0.$\n\n- Since the sine of the angle corresponds to the $y$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=1.$\n\nTherefore, using the fact that $\\operatorname{cot}\\theta =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cot}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})}{\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})}=\\frac{0}{1}=0.\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}\\pi +\\operatorname{cot}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=0+0=0.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233306 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "193f007a-0943-5ee4-b21d-1c12d8a9aa4c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512156} {:answer/id #uuid "346c7c5f-30ec-593f-bea5-24e469b9441b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512214} {:answer/id #uuid "654bd16e-0fb9-537f-a166-1c6dfab5eb1d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Undefined" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512341} {:answer/id #uuid "7580ba1f-3abd-5900-907d-0ec7cc95376e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512376} {:answer/id #uuid "a6855aa1-9c74-5c2d-8f0f-04a41e81ebd5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512494}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7580ba1f-3abd-5900-907d-0ec7cc95376e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512376} :answer-field/id #uuid "5809f205-7813-519f-9ba7-8f909067fdec" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512773}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "55febbab-96d0-5d10-a73b-1109a9b31684" :question/math-academy-id "q-123586" :question/problem "Find the value of $\\operatorname{sec}⁡0+\\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$" :question/worked-solution "The angles $0$ and $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ are quadrantal angles. Let's draw out the quadrantal angles on the unit circle.\n\n![](images/79/792a778286533d589a3410223aaf197b07e64ea77566042d7bb8ac13fbad9768.png)\n\n- Let's first consider the angle $0.$ From the unit circle, we see that the angle $0$ corresponds to the point $(1,0).$  Since the cosine of the angle corresponds to the $x$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{cos}⁡0=1.$ Therefore, using the fact that $\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sec}⁡0=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡0}=\\frac{1}{1}=1.\n$$\n\n- Now, let's consider the angle $\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ From the unit circle, we see that the angle $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ corresponds to the point $(0,1).$  Since the sine of the angle corresponds to the $y$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=1.$ Therefore, using the fact that $\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})}=\\frac{1}{1}=1.\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sec}⁡0+\\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=1+1=2.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233309 :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3b9c37b4-bc80-57e0-8c21-90f25b66d128" :question/math-academy-id "q-123602" :question/problem "$\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{\\pi }{6})=$" :question/worked-solution "Let's remind ourselves of the special trigonometric ratios, as given by the unit circle.\n\n![](images/db/dbf36192ac332dc49ed75278838e989a37ac7d888311be1b8b059a7b59667aa6.png)\n\nFrom the unit circle, we see that the angle $\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ corresponds to the point $(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},\\frac{1}{2}).$\n\nSince the cosine of the angle corresponds to the $x$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{\\pi }{6})=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233310 :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ec1d9fcb-300c-52db-b7dd-4284ae3c3eae" :question/math-academy-id "q-123603" :question/problem "$\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{5\\pi }{6})=$" :question/worked-solution "Let's remind ourselves of the special trigonometric ratios, as given by the unit circle.\n\n![](images/db/dbf36192ac332dc49ed75278838e989a37ac7d888311be1b8b059a7b59667aa6.png)\n\nFrom the unit circle, we see that the angle $\\frac{5\\pi }{6}$ corresponds to the point $(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},\\frac{1}{2}).$\n\nSince the sine of the angle corresponds to the $y$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{5\\pi }{6})=\\frac{1}{2}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233311 :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "51e0c6ac-58dd-5ea1-b05c-ba62077e2930" :question/math-academy-id "q-123606" :question/problem "$\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{7\\pi }{4})=$" :question/worked-solution "Let's remind ourselves of the special trigonometric ratios, as given by the unit circle.\n\n![](images/db/dbf36192ac332dc49ed75278838e989a37ac7d888311be1b8b059a7b59667aa6.png)\n\nFrom the unit circle, we see that the angle $\\frac{7\\pi }{4}$ corresponds to the point $(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}).$\n\nSince the cosine of the angle corresponds to the $x$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{7\\pi }{4})=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233312 :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "919e5fee-145b-582c-ab94-0d44db6ad8be" :question/math-academy-id "q-123607" :question/problem "$\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{\\pi }{6})=$" :question/worked-solution "Let's remind ourselves of the special trigonometric ratios, as given by the unit circle.\n\n![](images/db/dbf36192ac332dc49ed75278838e989a37ac7d888311be1b8b059a7b59667aa6.png)\n\nFrom the unit circle, we see that the angle $\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ corresponds to the point $(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},\\frac{1}{2}).$\n\nSince the sine of the angle corresponds to the $y$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{\\pi }{6})=\\frac{1}{2}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233313 :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "84a0eb74-4e3b-5b80-807e-ccf4f3502fe5" :question/math-academy-id "q-123608" :question/problem "$\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{7\\pi }{6})=$" :question/worked-solution "Let's remind ourselves of the special trigonometric ratios, as given by the unit circle.\n\n![](images/db/dbf36192ac332dc49ed75278838e989a37ac7d888311be1b8b059a7b59667aa6.png)\n\nFrom the unit circle, we see that the angle $\\frac{7\\pi }{6}$ corresponds to the point $(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},-\\frac{1}{2}).$\n\nSince the cosine of the angle corresponds to the $x$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{7\\pi }{6})=-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233317 :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0c27e331-e1ed-524a-b522-71b9ecf986f0" :question/math-academy-id "q-123613" :question/problem "$\\operatorname{sec}(\\frac{2\\pi }{3})=$" :question/worked-solution "Let's remind ourselves of the special trigonometric ratios, as given by the unit circle.\n\n![](images/db/dbf36192ac332dc49ed75278838e989a37ac7d888311be1b8b059a7b59667aa6.png)\n\nFrom the unit circle, we see that the angle $\\frac{2\\pi }{3}$ corresponds to the point $(-\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}).$\n\nSince the cosine of the angle corresponds to the $x$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{2\\pi }{3})=-\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\nTherefore, using the fact that $\\operatorname{sec}\\theta =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sec}(\\frac{2\\pi }{3})=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{2\\pi }{3})}=-2.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233318 :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6d9550cf-7833-530e-9136-1bff18e6b8e8" :question/math-academy-id "q-123614" :question/problem "$\\operatorname{csc}(\\frac{7\\pi }{4})=$" :question/worked-solution "Let's remind ourselves of the special trigonometric ratios, as given by the unit circle.\n\n![](images/db/dbf36192ac332dc49ed75278838e989a37ac7d888311be1b8b059a7b59667aa6.png)\n\nFrom the unit circle, we see that the angle $\\frac{7\\pi }{4}$ corresponds to the point $(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}).$\n\nSince the sine of the angle corresponds to the $y$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{7\\pi }{4})=-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.$\n\nTherefore, using the fact that $\\operatorname{csc}\\theta =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csc}(\\frac{7\\pi }{4})=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{7\\pi }{4})}=-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{2}}=-\\sqrt{2}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233319 :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0e77a4af-0077-55fd-8721-e9eb6958cb6e" :question/math-academy-id "q-123615" :question/problem "$\\operatorname{sec}(\\frac{5\\pi }{3})=$" :question/worked-solution "Let's remind ourselves of the special trigonometric ratios, as given by the unit circle.\n\n![](images/db/dbf36192ac332dc49ed75278838e989a37ac7d888311be1b8b059a7b59667aa6.png)\n\nFrom the unit circle, we see that the angle $\\frac{5\\pi }{3}$ corresponds to the point $(\\frac{1}{2},-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}).$\n\nSince the cosine of the angle corresponds to the $x$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{5\\pi }{3})=\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\nTherefore, using the fact that $\\operatorname{sec}\\theta =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sec}(\\frac{5\\pi }{3})=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{5\\pi }{3})}=2.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233320 :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e495c159-fdf6-5c0a-ae9b-329f683a6a37" :question/math-academy-id "q-123630" :question/problem "$\\operatorname{tan}(\\frac{3\\pi }{4})=$" :question/worked-solution "Let's remind ourselves of the special trigonometric ratios, as given by the unit circle.\n\n![](images/db/dbf36192ac332dc49ed75278838e989a37ac7d888311be1b8b059a7b59667aa6.png)\n\nFrom the unit circle, we see that the angle $\\frac{3\\pi }{4}$ corresponds to the point $(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}).$\n\n- Since the cosine of the angle corresponds to the $x$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{3\\pi }{4})=-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.$\n\n- Since the sine of the angle corresponds to the $y$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{3\\pi }{4})=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.$\n\nTherefore, using the fact that $\\operatorname{tan}\\theta =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}(\\frac{3\\pi }{4})=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{3\\pi }{4})}{\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{3\\pi }{4})}=\\frac{(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})}{(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})}=-1.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233321 :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "feb4cb6a-c439-51c9-b406-a5cf0450903c" :question/math-academy-id "q-123631" :question/problem "$\\operatorname{cot}(\\frac{\\pi }{6})=$" :question/worked-solution "Let's remind ourselves of the special trigonometric ratios, as given by the unit circle.\n\n![](images/db/dbf36192ac332dc49ed75278838e989a37ac7d888311be1b8b059a7b59667aa6.png)\n\nFrom the unit circle, we see that the angle $\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ corresponds to the point $(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},\\frac{1}{2}).$\n\n- Since the cosine of the angle corresponds to the $x$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{\\pi }{6})=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.$\n\n- Since the sine of the angle corresponds to the $y$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{\\pi }{6})=\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\nTherefore, using the fact that $\\operatorname{cot}\\theta =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cot}(\\frac{\\pi }{6})=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{\\pi }{6})}{\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{\\pi }{6})}=\\frac{(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})}{(\\frac{1}{2})}=\\sqrt{3}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233322 :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "553a7193-1687-54fa-bc9e-279324be71c2" :question/math-academy-id "q-123633" :question/problem "$\\operatorname{tan}(\\frac{2\\pi }{3})=$" :question/worked-solution "Let's remind ourselves of the special trigonometric ratios, as given by the unit circle.\n\n![](images/db/dbf36192ac332dc49ed75278838e989a37ac7d888311be1b8b059a7b59667aa6.png)\n\nFrom the unit circle, we see that the angle $\\frac{2\\pi }{3}$ corresponds to the point $(-\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}).$\n\n- Since the cosine of the angle corresponds to the $x$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{2\\pi }{3})=-\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\n- Since the sine of the angle corresponds to the $y$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{2\\pi }{3})=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.$\n\nTherefore, using the fact that $\\operatorname{tan}\\theta =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}(\\frac{2\\pi }{3})=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{2\\pi }{3})}{\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{2\\pi }{3})}=\\frac{(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})}{(-\\frac{1}{2})}=-\\sqrt{3}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233323 :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6753b809-58ce-5f9f-b4c9-7ff93affbeb1" :question/math-academy-id "q-123636" :question/problem "$\\operatorname{tan}(\\frac{7\\pi }{4})=$" :question/worked-solution "Let's remind ourselves of the special trigonometric ratios, as given by the unit circle.\n\n![](images/db/dbf36192ac332dc49ed75278838e989a37ac7d888311be1b8b059a7b59667aa6.png)\n\nFrom the unit circle, we see that the angle $\\frac{7\\pi }{4}$ corresponds to the point $(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}).$\n\n- Since the cosine of the angle corresponds to the $x$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{7\\pi }{4})=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.$\n\n- Since the sine of the angle corresponds to the $y$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{7\\pi }{4})=-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.$\n\nTherefore, using the fact that $\\operatorname{tan}\\theta =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}(\\frac{7\\pi }{4})=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{7\\pi }{4})}{\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{7\\pi }{4})}=\\frac{(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})}{(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})}=-1.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233324 :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2d02f521-e8ed-522a-a329-1f6d01f412b9" :question/math-academy-id "q-123638" :question/problem "$\\operatorname{tan}(\\frac{\\pi }{3})=$" :question/worked-solution "Let's remind ourselves of the special trigonometric ratios, as given by the unit circle.\n\n![](images/db/dbf36192ac332dc49ed75278838e989a37ac7d888311be1b8b059a7b59667aa6.png)\n\nFrom the unit circle, we see that the angle $\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ corresponds to the point $(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}).$\n\n- Since the cosine of the angle corresponds to the $x$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{\\pi }{3})=\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\n- Since the sine of the angle corresponds to the $y$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{\\pi }{3})=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.$\n\nTherefore, using the fact that $\\operatorname{tan}\\theta =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}(\\frac{\\pi }{3})=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{\\pi }{3})}{\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{\\pi }{3})}=\\frac{(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})}{(\\frac{1}{2})}=\\sqrt{3}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233327 :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7e9ddeaa-d255-5a72-8677-7945bfdc7ec5" :question/math-academy-id "q-123832" :question/problem "Given that  ${a}_{n}=\\frac{1}{n!}$ for $n\\ge 1,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. - $\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}\\ge 1$ for all $n\\ge 1$\nII. - ${a}_{n}$ is an increasing sequence\nIII. - ${a}_{n}$ is a decreasing sequence" :question/worked-solution "Suppose the sequence ${a}_{n}$ is a positive sequence defined for $n\\ge 1,$ and that\n\n$$\nL=\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}.\n$$\n\nThen,\n\n- if $L\\ge 1$ for all $n\\ge 1,$ then ${a}_{n}$ is *increasing,* and\n\n- if $0<L\\le 1$ for all $n\\ge 1,$ then ${a}_{n}$ is *decreasing.*\n\nNotice that, in this case, ${a}_{n}=\\frac{1}{n!}$ is positive for all $n\\ge 1.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's analyze each statement.\n\n- Statement I is false. Computing the ratio $L,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}} & =\\frac{(\\frac{1}{(n+1)!})}{(\\frac{1}{n!})} \\\\  & =\\frac{n!}{(n+1)!} \\\\  & =\\frac{n!}{(n+1)\\cdot n!} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{n!}}{(n+1)\\cdot \\cancel{n!}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{n+1} \\\\  & \\le 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false whereas statement III is true. Since $0\\le L<1$ for all $n\\ge 1,$ we can conclude that the sequence ${a}_{n}$ is decreasing.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233331 :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8d3e475a-31df-5b75-aaa4-5e6fc5ca47ec" :question/math-academy-id "q-123953" :question/problem "Determine the third partial sum of the series\n${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{(\\frac{3}{4})}^{n}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let ${a}_{n}={(\\frac{3}{4})}^{n}.$ We note that this is a geometric sequence with $r=\\frac{3}{4}$ and first term given by\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}={(\\frac{3}{4})}^{1}=\\frac{3}{4}.\n$$\n\nThe $k$th partial sum of a geometric series is given by\n\n$$\n{s}_{k}={a}_{1}(\\frac{1-{r}^{k}}{1-r}).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the third partial sum is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{s}_{3}={\\sum }_{n=1}^{3}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot \\frac{(1-{r}^{3})}{1-r} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}\\cdot \\frac{(1-{[\\frac{3}{4}]}^{3})}{(1-\\frac{3}{4})} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}\\cdot \\frac{(1-\\frac{27}{64})}{(\\frac{1}{4})} \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\cancel{\\frac{1}{4}}\\cdot \\frac{(\\frac{37}{64})}{(\\cancel{\\frac{1}{4}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{111}{64}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233333 :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "3fa0e027-8b11-500a-8c90-42e31bd2cca3" :question/math-academy-id "q-123969" :question/problem "Consider the series ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi ).$ Does it converge or diverge? If it converges, what is the sum of the series?" :question/worked-solution "Let's compute the first few partial sums:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{s}_{1} & =\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{3\\pi }{2})=-1 \\\\ {s}_{2} & =-1+\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{5\\pi }{2})=0 \\\\ {s}_{3} & =0+\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{7\\pi }{2})=-1 \\\\ {s}_{4} & =-1+\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{9\\pi }{2})=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the above, we can see that the sequence of partial sums oscillates between $0$ and $-1$ forever. \n\nSo the limit of the sequence of partial sums does not exist, and therefore the series is divergent."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233336 :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6f1a0a81-e526-5dc6-a0b8-85fe14c6cf8d" :question/math-academy-id "q-124064" :question/problem "If ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}=-\\frac{3}{4}$, \nthen calculate ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }8{a}_{n}.$" :question/worked-solution "Taking the constant factor outside of the summation sign gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }8{a}_{n} & =8{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n} \\\\  & =8(-\\frac{3}{4}) \\\\  & =-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233348 :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "1c8ad511-8d6e-54c5-9eb8-ac78d4cee4dd" :question/math-academy-id "q-124539" :question/problem "What is $\\operatorname{tan}(-\\frac{17\\pi }{12})$ expressed in terms of a reference angle?" :question/worked-solution "We wish to express $\\operatorname{tan}(-\\frac{17\\pi }{12})$ in terms of $\\operatorname{tan}{\\theta }_{R},$ where ${\\theta }_{R}$ is the reference angle corresponding to the angle $\\theta =-\\frac{17\\pi }{12}.$ \n\nTo express $\\operatorname{tan}(-\\frac{17\\pi }{12})$ in terms of $\\operatorname{tan}{\\theta }_{R},$ we proceed as follows: \n\n- **Step 1**: Find the corresponding coterminal angle that lies in the range $[0,2\\pi ).$ We do this by adding integer multiples of $2\\pi $ until we get our desired angle:\n\n$$\n-\\frac{17\\pi }{12}+2\\pi =\\frac{7\\pi }{12}\\,✓\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}(-\\frac{17\\pi }{12})=\\operatorname{tan}(\\frac{7\\pi }{12}).\n$$\n\n![](images/78/7828b40e3fba874611bb9c20e38e0880375aec389ae8ec646d92ea1e1b276883.png)\n\n- **Step 2**: Compute the reference angle. Since $\\theta =\\frac{7\\pi }{12}$ is in the $2$nd quadrant, the reference angle is\n\n$$\n{\\theta }_{R}=\\pi -\\frac{7\\pi }{12}=\\frac{5\\pi }{12}.\n$$\n\n- **Step 3**: Determine the correct sign. The tangent function is negative $(-)$ in the second quadrant. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}(-\\frac{17\\pi }{12})=-\\operatorname{tan}{\\theta }_{R}=-\\operatorname{tan}(\\frac{5\\pi }{12})\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}(-\\frac{17\\pi }{12})=-\\operatorname{tan}(\\frac{5\\pi }{12}).\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233350 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "14bf80a3-1665-5977-9753-8899ce180734" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528352} {:answer/id #uuid "631ca0b5-ae05-5843-8fce-eaa43004c3a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528534} {:answer/id #uuid "8fbbebd6-1052-5e30-bf33-b4a804d2efb5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-17" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528644} {:answer/id #uuid "e9c2927b-5f83-5559-9be7-b5aabb0973ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528878} {:answer/id #uuid "fb0d9032-620e-5573-8ccc-ca1cd595057d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-21" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528923}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fb0d9032-620e-5573-8ccc-ca1cd595057d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-21" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528923} :answer-field/id #uuid "3091e31c-4430-5ffe-b4b7-0a02be1e4edf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528969}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "cf1f4594-bcb0-53bd-9558-7de1afb93bf3" :question/math-academy-id "q-124577" :question/problem "Given that ${a}_{1}=-14$ and $\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}=\\frac{1}{3}$ for all integers $n\\ge 1,$ find the value of ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}.$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given series is geometric series because the ratio of any two consecutive terms is constant.\n\nThe sum to infinity ${S}_{\\infty }$ of a geometric series is given by the formula\n\n$$\n{S}_{\\infty }=\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r},\\,|r|<1,\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{1}$ is the first term and $r$ is the common ratio.\n\nWe're told that the first term is\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=-14.\n$$\n\nThe common ratio is\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}=\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\nSo, the sum of the series is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n} & =\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r} \\\\  & =\\frac{-14}{(1-\\frac{1}{3})} \\\\  & =-\\frac{14}{(\\frac{2}{3})} \\\\  & =-14\\cdot \\frac{3}{2} \\\\  & =-21.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233352 :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1e6adbb9-567d-56de-b218-92109b0b666a" :question/math-academy-id "q-124582" :question/problem "${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }15{(\\frac{2}{3})}^{n}=$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given series is a geometric series because it is in the form $\\sum a{r}^{n}.$ \n\nThe sum to infinity ${S}_{\\infty }$ of a geometric series is given by the formula\n\n$$\n{S}_{\\infty }=\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r},\\,|r|<1,\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{1}$ is the first term and $r$ is the common ratio.\n\nThe first term is\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=15{(\\frac{2}{3})}^{1}=10.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe common ratio is\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}}=\\frac{15{(\\frac{2}{3})}^{2}}{15{(\\frac{2}{3})}^{1}}=\\frac{2}{3}.\n$$\n\nSo, the sum of the series is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }15{(\\frac{2}{3})}^{n} & =\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r} \\\\  & =\\frac{10}{(1-\\frac{2}{3})} \\\\  & =\\frac{10}{(\\frac{1}{3})} \\\\  & =10\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =30.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233353 :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e6f88391-d5bf-5c6b-a90e-794e84eeaae1" :question/math-academy-id "q-124585" :question/problem "${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }15{(\\frac{2}{5})}^{n}=$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given series is a geometric series because it is in the form $\\sum a{r}^{n}.$ \n\nThe sum to infinity ${S}_{\\infty }$ of a geometric series is given by the formula\n\n$$\n{S}_{\\infty }=\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r},\\,|r|<1,\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{1}$ is the first term and $r$ is the common ratio.\n\nThe first term is\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=15{(\\frac{2}{5})}^{1}=6.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe common ratio is\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}}=\\frac{15{(\\frac{2}{5})}^{2}}{15{(\\frac{2}{5})}^{1}}=\\frac{2}{5}.\n$$\n\nSo, the sum of the series is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }5{(\\frac{2}{5})}^{n} & =\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{(1-\\frac{2}{5})} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{(\\frac{3}{5})} \\\\  & =6\\cdot \\frac{5}{3} \\\\  & =10.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233354 :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "88fadb3e-21de-57ff-bf0c-a87ee017fb37" :question/math-academy-id "q-124586" :question/problem "${\\sum }_{n=4}^{\\infty }16{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{n}=$" :question/worked-solution "First, note that the index $n$ starts from $4.$ However, it is still a geometric series because it is in the form $\\sum a{r}^{n}.$ \n\nIn this case, the first term is ${a}_{4},$ and so the sum to infinity ${S}_{\\infty }$ of this geometric series is given by\n\n$$\n{S}_{\\infty }=\\frac{{a}_{4}}{1-r},\\,|r|<1.\n$$\n\nFor the first few terms, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{4} & =16{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{4}=16(\\frac{1}{16})=1 \\\\ {a}_{5} & =16{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{5}=16(\\frac{1}{32})=\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe common ratio is\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{{a}_{5}}{{a}_{4}}=\\frac{(\\frac{1}{2})}{1}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nSo, the sum of the series is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=4}^{\\infty }16{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{n} & =\\frac{{a}_{4}}{1-r} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(1-\\frac{1}{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(\\frac{1}{2})} \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\frac{2}{1} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233355 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0439c9c0-9b23-5040-ba96-004238c51a3f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186600969} {:answer/id #uuid "1f3d4b62-a262-5eec-b433-e1e3e075e9e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186601007} {:answer/id #uuid "201ad891-dce1-53ed-9e8f-85b1bc98ccc5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "30" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186601009} {:answer/id #uuid "3fe994da-9e10-532f-9317-e074c4cce71f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186601058} {:answer/id #uuid "b1fe0365-fb4b-5296-90e1-50e883e339ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186601213}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0439c9c0-9b23-5040-ba96-004238c51a3f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186600969} :answer-field/id #uuid "cf33de49-36d5-5682-9940-fe973952b13a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186601421}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "bdf2f2e3-8886-5a92-82ab-fa99fe28255c" :question/math-academy-id "q-124587" :question/problem "Evaluate the series ${\\sum }_{n=2}^{\\infty }75{(\\frac{2}{5})}^{n}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, note that the index $n$ starts from $2.$ However, it is still a geometric series because it is in the form $\\sum a{r}^{n}.$ \n\nIn this case, the first term is ${a}_{2},$ and so the sum to infinity ${S}_{\\infty }$ of this geometric series is given by\n\n$$\n{S}_{\\infty }=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{1-r},\\,|r|<1.\n$$\n\nFor the first few terms, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{2} & =75{(\\frac{2}{5})}^{2}=12 \\\\ {a}_{3} & =75{(\\frac{2}{5})}^{3}=\\frac{24}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe common ratio is\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{{a}_{3}}{{a}_{2}}=\\frac{(\\frac{24}{5})}{12}=\\frac{2}{5}.\n$$\n\nSo, the sum of the series is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=2}^{\\infty }75{(\\frac{2}{5})}^{n} & =\\frac{{a}_{2}}{1-r} \\\\  & =\\frac{12}{(1-\\frac{2}{5})} \\\\  & =\\frac{12}{(\\frac{3}{5})} \\\\  & =20.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233381 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "10e0df1d-e35f-5855-a834-1c94f1908569" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pm \\frac{z}{5y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481585} {:answer/id #uuid "7c3d666f-f045-5443-9c6d-274e5c6211c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{|z|}{5y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481612} {:answer/id #uuid "b1faed5d-4a27-57fd-9b17-4d064369014c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{z}{5y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481621} {:answer/id #uuid "dbac0fc6-9501-50df-88fb-33580ce66e8f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{z}^{2}}{5{y}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481635} {:answer/id #uuid "e35cb55d-2dfd-55eb-bf0f-6fa62c65efa2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{|z|}{5|y|}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481639}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e35cb55d-2dfd-55eb-bf0f-6fa62c65efa2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{|z|}{5|y|}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481639} :answer-field/id #uuid "b6c4f3e7-b119-592e-9f35-31368c3f7121" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481660}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "97082509-6da1-5d7c-a3a4-921738c6eb05" :question/math-academy-id "q-129471" :question/problem "$\\sqrt{\\frac{{z}^{2}}{25{y}^{2}}}=$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the square root of a perfect square can be simplified using absolute value.\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{{x}^{2}}=|x|\n$$\n\nAlso, we notice that ${z}^{2},25,$ and ${y}^{2}$ are all perfect squares.\n\nFirst, we write the expression under the square root as a perfect square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{\\frac{{z}^{2}}{25{y}^{2}}} & =\\sqrt{\\frac{{z}^{2}}{{5}^{2}\\cdot {y}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{z\\cdot z}{5\\cdot 5\\cdot y\\cdot y}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{z\\cdot z}{5\\cdot y\\cdot 5\\cdot y}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{z}{5y}\\cdot \\frac{z}{5y}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{z}{5y})}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we take the absolute value of the squared quantity and simplify using the rules of absolute value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{(\\frac{z}{5y})}^{2}} & =|\\frac{z}{5y}| \\\\  & =\\frac{|z|}{|5y|} \\\\  & =\\frac{|z|}{|5|\\cdot |y|} \\\\  & =\\frac{|z|}{5|y|}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233386 :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "8fb85bda-497e-5c77-a76c-263397e57119" :question/math-academy-id "q-131072" :question/problem "Solve $\\frac{\\sqrt{3{v}^{2}-4v+12}}{2}-2=0.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we isolate the term $\\frac{\\sqrt{3{v}^{2}-4v+12}}{2}$ by adding $2$ to both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{3{v}^{2}-4v+12}}{2}-2 & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{3{v}^{2}-4v+12}}{2} & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we isolate the square root by multiplying by $2$ on both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{3{v}^{2}-4v+12}}{2} & =2 \\\\ \\sqrt{3{v}^{2}-4v+12} & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the square root is isolated, we can proceed as usual. Squaring both sides of the equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{3{v}^{2}-4v+12} & =4 \\\\ (\\sqrt{3{v}^{2}-4v+12})^{2} & ={4}^{2} \\\\ 3{v}^{2}-4v+12 & =16 \\\\ 3{v}^{2}-4v-4 & =0 \\\\ (3v+2)(v-2) & =0 \\\\ v & =-\\frac{2}{3},\\,2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's check the result by plugging it into the original equation:\n\n- For $v=-\\frac{2}{3},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{3{(-\\frac{2}{3})}^{2}-4(-\\frac{2}{3})+12}}{2}-2 & \\overset{?}{=}0 \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{\\frac{4}{3}+\\frac{8}{3}+12}}{2}-2 & \\overset{?}{=}0 \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{16}}{2}-2 & \\overset{?}{=}0 \\\\ \\frac{4}{2}-2 & \\overset{?}{=}0 \\\\ 2-2 & \\overset{?}{=}0 \\\\ 0 & =0\\,\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For $v=2,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{3({2}^{2})-4(2)+12}}{2}-2 & \\overset{?}{=}0 \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{12-8+12}}{2}-2 & \\overset{?}{=}0 \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{16}}{2}-2 & \\overset{?}{=}0 \\\\ \\frac{4}{2}-2 & \\overset{?}{=}0 \\\\ 2-2 & \\overset{?}{=}0 \\\\ 0 & =0\\,\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBoth $v=-\\frac{2}{3}$ and $v=2$ satisfy the original equation, so we can accept them both as solutions.\n\nTherefore, the solutions to $\\frac{\\sqrt{3{v}^{2}-4v+12}}{2}-2=0$ are $v=-\\frac{2}{3}$ and $v=2.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233414 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "99255c4c-5558-5417-ba91-7c0183769869" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232162}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "99255c4c-5558-5417-ba91-7c0183769869" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232162} :answer-field/id #uuid "be028048-638b-5cc3-bf05-4c507685304c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232161}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6a70ed2f-24be-520f-bc29-361c3cb620d9" :question/math-academy-id "q-133321" :question/problem "Find ${lim}_{x\\to \\,\\pi }f(x).$ If the limit does not exist, write \"DNE\" (without the quotation marks).\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\,\\pi }f(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Substituting $x=\\pi $ directly into the limit, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,\\pi }f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\,\\pi }6\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{x}{3}) \\\\  & =6\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =6\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =6\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233415 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fd93faec-2150-5f36-b61a-38ef54bfd10d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "DNE" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232164}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fd93faec-2150-5f36-b61a-38ef54bfd10d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "DNE" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232164} :answer-field/id #uuid "2b1cdb16-5d8d-5a62-a087-0ed8f98ddd07" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232163}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9bc1c9e9-c3fa-5468-93cc-68237790e0b0" :question/math-academy-id "q-133322" :question/problem "Find ${lim}_{x\\to \\,-\\infty }f(x).$ If the limit does not exist, write \"DNE\" (without the quotation marks).\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\,-\\infty }f(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let's sketch the graph of $y=f(x)=6\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{x}{3}).$\n\n![](images/72/72a0459ee5db59562a19961d0304785fb8d46db8be24ad7839d4b8690b0dc835.png)\n\nAs $x$ decreases to $-\\infty $, the graph oscillates between the values $y=-6$ and $y=6$ indefinitely. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,-\\infty }f(x)={lim}_{x\\to \\,-\\infty }6\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{x}{3})=DNE.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233416 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6b5e4c82-03ad-5613-8969-930796d66c46" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6}{h}\\operatorname{sin}(\\pi +\\frac{h}{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232166}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6b5e4c82-03ad-5613-8969-930796d66c46" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6}{h}\\operatorname{sin}(\\pi +\\frac{h}{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232166} :answer-field/id #uuid "dc163ba8-f876-5afb-aa02-7c24d1ad0781" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232165}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "aba4b4b3-386a-57af-b8b5-eafdd6f93386" :question/math-academy-id "q-133323" :question/problem "What is the average rate of change of the function $f(x)$ over the interval $x\\in [3\\pi ,3\\pi +h],$ where $h>0$ is a parameter? Express your answer in terms of $h.$\n\n$\\frac{Δf}{Δx}\\,=\\,$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We have the function $f(x)=6\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{x}{3}).$ Notice that we are given the length $h$ of the interval. So, the average rate of change of $f$ over this interval is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{Δf}{Δx}=\\frac{f(a+h)-f(a)}{h},\n$$\n\nwith $a=3\\pi .$ Substituting into the formula and simplifying gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{Δf}{Δx} & =\\frac{f(a+h)-f(a)}{h} \\\\  & =\\frac{f(3\\pi +h)-f(3\\pi )}{h} \\\\  & =\\frac{6\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{1}{3}(3\\pi +h))-6\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot 3\\pi )}{h} \\\\  & =\\frac{6\\operatorname{sin}(\\pi +\\frac{h}{3})-6\\operatorname{sin}(\\pi )}{h} \\\\  & =\\frac{6\\operatorname{sin}(\\pi +\\frac{h}{3})-6(0)}{h} \\\\  & =\\frac{6\\operatorname{sin}(\\pi +\\frac{h}{3})}{h} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{h}\\operatorname{sin}(\\pi +\\frac{h}{3})\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233418 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "25133b7b-5236-5b36-91ef-d3e47673169f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232170}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "25133b7b-5236-5b36-91ef-d3e47673169f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232170} :answer-field/id #uuid "6d54f1e9-8080-50cc-99bd-ab2a6b4c9b21" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232169}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3cbafae4-2598-54df-ac1f-4d1b38dbf7e6" :question/math-academy-id "q-133325" :question/problem "Find ${lim}_{x\\to \\,(3\\pi )^{-}}\\frac{1}{f(x)}.$ If the limit does not exist, write \"DNE\" (without the quotation marks).\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\,(3\\pi )^{-}}\\frac{1}{f(x)}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, let's attempt to evaluate the limit by direct substitution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (3\\pi )^{-}}\\frac{1}{f(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to (3\\pi )^{-}}\\frac{1}{6\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{x}{3})} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{3\\pi }{3})} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6\\operatorname{sin}(\\pi )} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{0},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is undefined.\n\nTherefore, to find the limit, let's sketch the graph of $y=\\frac{1}{6\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{x}{3})}=\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{csc}(\\frac{x}{3}).$\n\n![](images/42/42869c56604cbec85b5e4915f77b42e72444b7d9d117e140d9eba56b58c0f131.png)\n\nFrom the graph, as $x$ approaches $3\\pi $ from the left, the values of $y=\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{csc}(\\frac{x}{3})$ are positive and increase without bound. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (3\\pi )^{-}}\\frac{1}{f(x)}={lim}_{x\\to \\,(3\\pi )^{-}}\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{csc}(\\frac{x}{3})=\\infty .\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233420 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2ab7b9a9-97dc-5def-9fe8-f610c73625f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232174}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2ab7b9a9-97dc-5def-9fe8-f610c73625f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232174} :answer-field/id #uuid "47b642f1-0b89-5fae-ae37-34937e75b5da" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232173}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a350247e-7ecd-5d52-833a-6128b4df7d84" :question/math-academy-id "q-133327" :question/problem "Find ${lim}_{x\\to \\,2}\\frac{(x-2)f(x)}{{x}^{2}-4}.$ If the limit does not exist, write \"DNE\" (without the quotation marks).\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\,2}\\frac{(x-2)f(x)}{{x}^{2}-4}=$\n{{field-1}}$\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{2}{3})$" :question/worked-solution "Previously, we found that \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,2}\\frac{x-2}{{x}^{2}-4}=\\frac{1}{4}.\n$$\n\n \n\nApplying the product rule to the original limit, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,2}\\frac{(x-2)f(x)}{{x}^{2}-4} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\,2}[\\frac{x-2}{{x}^{2}-4}\\cdot f(x)] \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\,2}\\frac{x-2}{{x}^{2}-4}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\,2}f(x) \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\,2}\\frac{x-2}{{x}^{2}-4}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\,2}6\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{x}{3}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{4})\\cdot 6\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{2}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{2}{3})\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233422 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a5acf83a-caaa-5169-be3f-4396f340e3a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4+\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232178}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a5acf83a-caaa-5169-be3f-4396f340e3a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4+\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232178} :answer-field/id #uuid "24ba2edc-60a0-5111-8e1f-e2c5236c6e13" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232177}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a50a260c-43fe-5579-b541-bb31003721ea" :question/math-academy-id "q-133329" :question/problem "Find the third partial sum ${s}_{3}$ of the series ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{f(6n\\pi +\\pi )}{(\\sqrt{3})^{n}}.$\n\n${s}_{3}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let's start by calculating the first three terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} & =\\frac{f(6\\pi \\cdot 1+\\pi )}{(\\sqrt{3})^{1}}=\\frac{f(7\\pi )}{\\sqrt{3}}=\\frac{6\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{7\\pi }{3})}{\\sqrt{3}}=\\frac{6\\sqrt{3}}{2\\cdot \\sqrt{3}}=3 \\\\ {a}_{2} & =\\frac{f(6\\pi \\cdot 2+\\pi )}{(\\sqrt{3})^{2}}=\\frac{f(13\\pi )}{3}=\\frac{6\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{13\\pi }{3})}{3}=\\frac{6\\sqrt{3}}{2\\cdot 3}=\\sqrt{3} \\\\ {a}_{3} & =\\frac{f(6\\pi \\cdot 3+\\pi )}{(\\sqrt{3})^{3}}=\\frac{f(19\\pi )}{3\\sqrt{3}}=\\frac{6\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{19\\pi }{3})}{3\\sqrt{3}}=\\frac{6\\sqrt{3}}{2\\cdot 3\\sqrt{3}}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the third partial sum is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{s}_{3} & ={a}_{1}+{a}_{2}+{a}_{3} \\\\  & =3+\\sqrt{3}+1 \\\\  & =4+\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233423 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4541aede-24e3-55bf-b6d5-c3d9310def60" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{\\sqrt{3}-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232180}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4541aede-24e3-55bf-b6d5-c3d9310def60" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{\\sqrt{3}-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232180} :answer-field/id #uuid "50e514d3-a948-599d-bfa7-67a5488c061b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232179}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2d13daa6-87fd-5dec-89b3-0bd50ec97fca" :question/math-academy-id "q-133330" :question/problem "Given that ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{f(6n\\pi +\\pi )}{(\\sqrt{3})^{n}}$ is an infinite geometric series, compute its sum.\n\n${S}_{\\infty }=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Previously, we evaluated the first few terms of the series:\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=3,\\,{a}_{2}=\\sqrt{3},\\,{a}_{3}=1\n$$\n\nThe sum to infinity ${S}_{\\infty }$ of a geometric series is given by the formula\n\n$$\n{S}_{\\infty }=\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r},\\,|r|<1,\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{1}$ is the first term, and $r$ is the common ratio.\n\nFor the given geometric series, the first term is ${a}_{1}=3,$ and the common ratio is\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}}=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}.\n$$\n\nSo, using the formula, we find that the sum to infinity is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{\\infty } & =\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{1-(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}-1}{\\sqrt{3}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{\\sqrt{3}-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233441 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "63746cd7-d78b-55d9-9dd3-7e1c9980ab08" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{x}^{2}-7x+3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503174} {:answer/id #uuid "8b920ce3-ff7b-5bb6-8fdf-35583cef9643" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}+6x+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503263} {:answer/id #uuid "a0187cce-8b24-5027-bfa7-f2e2f49ae2ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{x}^{2}-5x+3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503301} {:answer/id #uuid "a3e1935b-7c69-571c-988f-1bd72cb529a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}+6x-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503311} {:answer/id #uuid "f352bcca-38a2-5ecb-8851-59eb03ee8d27" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}+7x+3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503477}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f352bcca-38a2-5ecb-8851-59eb03ee8d27" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}+7x+3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503477} :answer-field/id #uuid "968ccea1-eddc-5df5-b531-eb97516921db" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503590}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3ac66977-fb7e-55ea-b01a-306cc1fa6e11" :question/math-academy-id "q-138848" :question/problem "Consider the function $y=\\sqrt{({x}^{2}+6x){sin}^{2}⁡x}.$  The derivative of this function for $x\\in (0,\\pi )$ is given by\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\frac{g(x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+h(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+6x}}.\n$$\n\nFind the function $g(x)+h(x).$" :question/worked-solution "Our function can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\sqrt{({x}^{2}+6x){sin}^{2}⁡x} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+6x}\\cdot \\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡x} \\\\  & =({x}^{2}+6x)^{1/2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we take the natural logarithm of both sides and use the product and power rules for logarithms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(({x}^{2}+6x)^{1/2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(({x}^{2}+6x)^{1/2})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+6x)+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+6x)+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)) \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot {y}^{′} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+6x}\\cdot (2x+6)+\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{′}}{y} & =\\frac{x+3}{{x}^{2}+6x}+\\operatorname{cot}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we solve for ${y}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =y(\\frac{x+3}{{x}^{2}+6x}+\\operatorname{cot}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $y=\\sqrt{({x}^{2}+6x){sin}^{2}⁡x}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =\\sqrt{({x}^{2}+6x){sin}^{2}⁡x}(\\frac{x+3}{{x}^{2}+6x}+\\operatorname{cot}⁡x) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{({x}^{2}+6x){sin}^{2}⁡x}(\\frac{x+3}{{x}^{2}+6x}+\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{({x}^{2}+6x){sin}^{2}⁡x}(\\frac{(x+3)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+({x}^{2}+6x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{({x}^{2}+6x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{|\\operatorname{sin}⁡x|}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\frac{(x+3)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+({x}^{2}+6x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+6x}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nGiven that $x\\in (0,\\pi ),$ then $|\\operatorname{sin}⁡x|=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x,$ so the fraction $\\frac{|\\operatorname{sin}⁡x|}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}$ simplifies to $1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =\\frac{(x+3)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+({x}^{2}+6x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+6x}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\ng(x)+h(x)=x+3+{x}^{2}+6x={x}^{2}+7x+3.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233443 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0afd21a9-c1f9-5631-b042-8d95195cced4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$L=\\infty .$ The series is divergent." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527329} {:answer/id #uuid "0ce64f0d-e9ba-5dbb-9ffc-282418bc0004" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$L=3.$ The series is divergent." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527333} {:answer/id #uuid "106587e7-d829-54ab-8b7e-609c77cf0a14" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$L=1.$ The series is divergent." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527340} {:answer/id #uuid "1b339991-d53a-533c-8056-20f5d713d32d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$L=0.$The series is convergent." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527365} {:answer/id #uuid "8ba6070b-da1a-5e0b-a72d-fc13af7e3165" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$L=1.$ The root test gives no conclusion." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527584}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8ba6070b-da1a-5e0b-a72d-fc13af7e3165" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$L=1.$ The root test gives no conclusion." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527584} :answer-field/id #uuid "ef334055-d235-506e-a901-ee66a80f4e9e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527902}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "16eb9f4b-4d3a-531a-a851-09494af3ba39" :question/math-academy-id "q-138998" :question/problem "Calculate $L={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\sqrt[n]{{a}_{n}}$ for the sequence ${a}_{n}=\\frac{3{n}^{n}}{(1+n)^{n}}.$ According to the root test, does ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}$ converge or diverge?" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have the series ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}$ such that ${a}_{n}\\ge 0$ for all $n\\ge 1,$ and that $L$ is defined as  \n\n$$\nL={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\sqrt[n]{{a}_{n}}={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{({a}_{n})}^{1/n}.\n$$\n\n \n\nThen, the root test states the following:\n\n- If $L<1$, the series converges.\n\n- If $L>1$ or $L=\\infty $, the series diverges.\n\n- If $L=1$, the root test is inconclusive.\n\nWe have the sequence ${a}_{n},$ defined as\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=\\frac{3{n}^{n}}{(1+n)^{n}}.\n$$\n\nTo apply the root test, we find the limit of $\\sqrt[n]{{a}_{n}}$ as $n\\to \\infty :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\sqrt[n]{{a}_{n}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{({a}_{n})}^{1/n} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{(\\frac{3{n}^{n}}{(1+n)^{n}})}^{1/n} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\sqrt[n]{3}\\,n}{1+n} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\sqrt[n]{3}\\,n}{n(\\frac{1}{n}+1)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\sqrt[n]{3}}{(\\frac{1}{n}+1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{0+1} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we used the fact that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\sqrt[n]{3}=1.\n$$\n\nSince $L=1,$ the root test gives no conclusion."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233444 :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "97b0bb65-2c6f-5b5b-acc3-37f3a7519268" :question/math-academy-id "q-14060" :question/problem "Calculate the sum to infinity of the geometric series $40+20+10+5+⋯$" :question/worked-solution "The sum to infinity ${S}_{\\infty }$ of a geometric series is given by the formula\n\n$$\n{S}_{\\infty }=\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r},\\,|r|<1,\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{1}$ is the first term and $r$ is the common ratio.\n\nFor the given geometric series, the first term is ${a}_{1}=40$ and the common ratio is\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}}=\\frac{20}{40}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nSo, using the formula, we find that the sum to infinity is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{\\infty } & =\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r} \\\\  & =\\frac{40}{(1-\\frac{1}{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{40}{(\\frac{1}{2})} \\\\  & =80.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233451 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "34647280-a3dc-5338-9da7-dde738d44c4a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269748} {:answer/id #uuid "63dbb56b-81f8-5a9e-a42f-d551d55ddb7d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270153} {:answer/id #uuid "69910eb5-561f-5cd3-b525-069fc7aa7d66" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270204} {:answer/id #uuid "9d5dd75d-ad46-5414-9a33-905eff9226d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270661} {:answer/id #uuid "cb508e58-5bcb-5836-85e6-e73bb1bfd11d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271055}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "34647280-a3dc-5338-9da7-dde738d44c4a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269748} :answer-field/id #uuid "3161c8fd-6e7b-5c92-87c1-62b7a8821ab9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271599}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "1e29af52-e62f-515a-a6c0-ecb0bc3bcf41" :question/math-academy-id "q-141281" :question/problem "$\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+\\frac{2\\pi }{3})=$" :question/worked-solution "Recall the sum formula for sine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u+v)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v+\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\n$$\n\nApplying the sum formula for sine with $u=x$ and $v=\\frac{2\\pi }{3},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+\\frac{2\\pi }{3}) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{3})+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2})+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\cdot (\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{2}+\\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233455 :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "609d2236-8468-565e-9ebe-8fd019ac095c" :question/math-academy-id "q-141380" :question/problem "$\\operatorname{cos}(x+\\frac{\\pi }{2})=$" :question/worked-solution "First, we recall the sum formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}(u+v)=\\operatorname{cos}u\\operatorname{cos}v-\\operatorname{sin}u\\operatorname{sin}v\n$$\n\nApplying the sum formula for cosine with $u=x$ and $v=\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}(x+\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =\\operatorname{cos}x\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})-\\operatorname{sin}x\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}x\\cdot 0-\\operatorname{sin}x\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233460 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b707a1d1-57fa-5736-811f-bf2ae96d060d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=8f(5x+2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512537} {:answer/id #uuid "c39f5368-56a8-56a6-9249-778f2951044b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=8f(\\frac{x}{5}+2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512567} {:answer/id #uuid "ce83393b-e04b-54a4-bf3c-968bb6cbc7d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=8f(5x)+16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512595} {:answer/id #uuid "ead58f74-c494-532c-8715-c931593791f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=8f(\\frac{x}{5})+16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512664} {:answer/id #uuid "fed01fe7-79b8-5045-81a9-f0e7af4fb8c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{8}f(\\frac{x}{5}+2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512716}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c39f5368-56a8-56a6-9249-778f2951044b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=8f(\\frac{x}{5}+2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512567} :answer-field/id #uuid "ffa36a73-cb38-5a3e-b22f-94ae7972e4d0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512862}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "3d424faa-a830-5c69-a1d8-32908ad96638" :question/math-academy-id "q-141752" :question/problem "Given the graph of $y=f(x),$ we apply the following transformations in the order given:\n\n1\\. Stretch it by a scale factor of $8$ parallel to the $y$-axis\n\n2\\. Stretch it by a scale factor of $5$ parallel to the $x$-axis\n\n3\\. Translate it to the left by $10$ units\n\nWhat is the final function expression after applying all the transformations?" :question/worked-solution "To find the expression, let's apply each of the transformations in turn.\n\n1\\. Stretching the graph of $y=f(x)$ by a scale factor of $8$ parallel to the $y$-axis is equivalent to multiplying the function expression by $8.$ Applying this transformation, we obtain \n\n  $$\n  y=8f(x).\n  $$\n\n2\\. Stretching the graph of $y=8f(x)$ by a scale factor of $5$ parallel to the $x$-axis is equivalent to replacing every instance of $x$ with $\\frac{1}{5}x.$ Applying this transformation, we obtain \n\n  $$\n  y=8f(\\frac{1}{5}x)=8f(\\frac{x}{5}).\n  $$\n\n3\\. Translating the graph of $y=8f(\\frac{x}{5})$ by $10$ units to the left is equivalent to replacing every instance of $x$ with $(x+10).$ Applying this transformation, we obtain \n\n  $$\n  y=8f(\\frac{1}{5}(x+10))=8f(\\frac{x}{5}+2).\n  $$\n\nTherefore, the final function expression is $y=8f(\\frac{x}{5}+2).$"}]
